window.QUESTION_BANK = [{"module": 1, "number": 1, "question": "<p>A company's objective is to achieve RM50 million profit. Unexpectedly strong demand increases profit to RM65 million.</p>\n<p>Under the broader definition of risk, this situation represents:</p>", "options": [["A", "No risk because profit increased"], ["B", "Risk because uncertainty caused deviation from the objective"], ["C", "Pure risk"], ["D", "Loss exposure only"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Risk is the effect of uncertainty on objectives and can involve positive deviation.</p>\n<p>The trap is thinking:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>positive outcome = no risk.</p>\n</blockquote>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 2, "question": "<p>A company knows that a machine may fail, operate normally, or operate at reduced capacity, and it can estimate probabilities for each outcome.</p>\n<p>This situation is best classified as:</p>", "options": [["A", "Uncertainty"], ["B", "Risk"], ["C", "Speculative uncertainty"], ["D", "Systemic risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Possible outcomes and probabilities are estimable.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk</span></div>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 3, "question": "<p>A newly invented technology could fundamentally reshape an industry, but companies do not yet know all the possible outcomes or their probabilities.</p>\n<p>This most closely represents:</p>", "options": [["A", "Pure risk"], ["B", "Market risk"], ["C", "Uncertainty"], ["D", "Loss reduction"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Possible outcomes themselves are unclear.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Uncertainty</span></div>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 4, "question": "<p>A warehouse is located in an earthquake zone, although no earthquake has occurred.</p>\n<p>The warehouse represents:</p>", "options": [["A", "Actual loss"], ["B", "Loss exposure"], ["C", "Loss reduction"], ["D", "Speculative risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Loss is possible even though none occurred.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Loss exposure</span></div>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 5, "question": "<p>A retailer experiences approximately 200 minor theft incidents every year.</p>\n<p>The \"200 incidents\" primarily measures:</p>", "options": [["A", "Severity"], ["B", "Frequency"], ["C", "Loss reduction"], ["D", "Systematic risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Number of incidents:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Frequency</span></div>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 6, "question": "<p>A manufacturer installs automatic sprinklers so that if fire occurs, damage is contained.</p>\n<p>This is:</p>", "options": [["A", "Avoidance"], ["B", "Loss prevention"], ["C", "Loss reduction"], ["D", "Risk retention"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Sprinklers operate after fire begins to reduce damage.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Severity} \\downarrow}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Loss reduction</span></div>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 7, "question": "<p>A manufacturer introduces safety training intended to decrease the number of workplace accidents.</p>\n<p>This is:</p>", "options": [["A", "Loss reduction"], ["B", "Loss prevention"], ["C", "Avoidance"], ["D", "Speculation"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Training aims to prevent accidents.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Frequency} \\downarrow}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Loss prevention</span></div>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 8, "question": "<p>A company decides not to enter a politically unstable country at all.</p>\n<p>This is:</p>", "options": [["A", "Loss prevention"], ["B", "Loss reduction"], ["C", "Avoidance"], ["D", "Diversification"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The exposure is never undertaken.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Avoidance</span></div>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 9, "question": "<p>Which is the clearest example of pure risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "Buying shares"], ["B", "Purchasing real estate hoping its price rises"], ["C", "A factory being damaged by fire"], ["D", "Entering a new foreign market"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Fire gives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{loss or no loss}$$</div>\n<p>No upside.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 10, "question": "<p>Which is the clearest speculative risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "Employee injury"], ["B", "Earthquake"], ["C", "Investing in shares"], ["D", "Theft"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Shares can produce:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{gain or loss}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore speculative.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 11, "question": "<p>Company A's CEO resigns unexpectedly, causing its share price to fall. Other firms are largely unaffected.</p>\n<p>This is primarily:</p>", "options": [["A", "Systematic risk"], ["B", "Non-diversifiable risk"], ["C", "Unsystematic risk"], ["D", "Market-wide risk"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>CEO departure affects one company.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Unsystematic / firm-specific</span></div>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 12, "question": "<p>Bank Negara unexpectedly raises interest rates and share prices across many industries decline.</p>\n<p>This is primarily:</p>", "options": [["A", "Diversifiable risk"], ["B", "Firm-specific risk"], ["C", "Systematic risk"], ["D", "Management risk"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Interest rates affect broad parts of the economy.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Systematic</span></div>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 13, "question": "<p>An investor owns 100 unrelated companies instead of one company.</p>\n<p>Which risk should be substantially reduced?</p>", "options": [["A", "Systematic risk"], ["B", "Unsystematic risk"], ["C", "Inflation risk"], ["D", "Interest-rate risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Diversification primarily reduces:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Unsystematic risk</span></div>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 14, "question": "<p>Which risk would generally <strong>not</strong> disappear through diversification?</p>", "options": [["A", "Employee strike at Company X"], ["B", "Lawsuit against Company Y"], ["C", "National recession"], ["D", "Failure of Company Z's new product"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A recession affects many companies in the same general direction.</p>\n<p>Therefore it cannot simply be diversified away.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 15, "question": "<p>A share has:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta = 1.4$$</div>\n<p>According to your chapter, this means the asset has:</p>", "options": [["A", "No systematic risk"], ["B", "Less systematic risk than average"], ["C", "More systematic risk than average"], ["D", "More unsystematic risk than average"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta&gt;1$$</div>\n<p>means above-average systematic risk according to your slides.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 16, "question": "<p>A payment-system outage prevents customers from completing transactions.</p>\n<p>The most immediate risk category is:</p>", "options": [["A", "Credit risk"], ["B", "Operational risk"], ["C", "Market risk"], ["D", "Speculative risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A technology/system failure affecting transactions is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Operational risk</span></div>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 17, "question": "<p>A bank's loan-approval model systematically approves borrowers who are unlikely to repay because the model is badly designed.</p>\n<p>The defective model itself represents:</p>", "options": [["A", "Market risk"], ["B", "Resource risk"], ["C", "Model/operational risk"], ["D", "Pure credit risk only"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The root cause is a flawed model.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Model risk / operational risk</span></div>\n<p>Credit losses may be the <strong>consequence</strong>.</p>\n<p>This root-cause distinction is exactly the sort of thing MCQs exploit.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 18, "question": "<p>A borrower cannot repay a RM500,000 loan.</p>\n<p>From the lender's perspective this is primarily:</p>", "options": [["A", "Market risk"], ["B", "Credit risk"], ["C", "Resource risk"], ["D", "Asset liquidity risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The borrower owes the lender money and may not repay.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Credit risk</span></div>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 19, "question": "<p>A company owns RM20 million of property but does not have enough cash to pay salaries due tomorrow.</p>\n<p>This primarily illustrates:</p>", "options": [["A", "Credit risk"], ["B", "Funding liquidity risk"], ["C", "Asset liquidity risk only"], ["D", "Market risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The company cannot meet immediate obligations.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Funding liquidity risk</span></div>\n<p>Do not confuse asset wealth with cash availability.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 20, "question": "<p>A company wants to sell a specialised asset immediately, but there are almost no buyers.</p>\n<p>This primarily illustrates:</p>", "options": [["A", "Funding liquidity risk"], ["B", "Asset liquidity risk"], ["C", "Credit risk"], ["D", "Business risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Difficulty selling an asset:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Asset liquidity risk</span></div>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 21, "question": "<p>A restaurant chain suffers a viral backlash after consumers object to the behaviour of one of its suppliers. Sales subsequently fall.</p>\n<p>What is the most accurate analysis?</p>", "options": [["A", "Only operational risk is present"], ["B", "Tangential reputational risk contributes to business risk"], ["C", "It is purely systematic risk"], ["D", "The supplier creates asset liquidity risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The supplier is a peripheral party, so the reputational risk is tangential under the chapter's terminology. The subsequent sales decline creates business consequences.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 22, "question": "<p>A company cuts quality-control spending to minimise costs. Product defects subsequently increase and customers begin leaving.</p>\n<p>Which chain best describes the situation?</p>", "options": [["A", "Credit risk → liquidity risk"], ["B", "Resource risk → systematic risk"], ["C", "Cost decision → quality risk → customer/business risk"], ["D", "Market risk → speculative risk"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The cost-cutting decision weakens quality control:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Quality problems → Customer loss → Business impact\"><span>Quality problems</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Customer loss</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Business impact</span></div>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 23, "question": "<p>A pharmaceutical company spends heavily on R&amp;D, but its new product fails commercially.</p>\n<p>According to the chapter's discussion of diversifiable risk, this would most closely represent:</p>", "options": [["A", "Systematic economic risk"], ["B", "Business/firm-specific risk"], ["C", "Non-diversifiable inflation risk"], ["D", "Pure risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>This is specific to the company's business activity rather than an economy-wide event.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 24, "question": "<p>A company has excellent assets but cannot convert them into cash quickly enough to meet immediate obligations.</p>\n<p>Which statement is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "The company is insolvent by definition"], ["B", "The company has no financial risk"], ["C", "The company can be asset-rich but liquidity-constrained"], ["D", "The situation must be credit risk"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>This is exactly why:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Assets} \\neq \\text{liquidity}$$</div>\n<p>A company may have substantial wealth but insufficient immediate cash.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 25, "question": "<p>Which statement best explains why investors are generally not compensated merely for taking high unsystematic risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "Unsystematic risk cannot cause losses"], ["B", "It can largely be reduced through diversification"], ["C", "Beta measures unsystematic risk"], ["D", "Systematic risk disappears in large portfolios"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>If investors can eliminate a risk relatively cheaply through diversification, simply choosing not to diversify does not make that exposure economically special.</p>\n<p>Your slides therefore emphasise systematic rather than unsystematic risk when discussing risk compensation.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 26, "question": "<p>A company uses a single supplier. The supplier suddenly shuts down, causing production to stop.</p>\n<p>Which answer identifies the <strong>most immediate underlying exposure</strong>?</p>", "options": [["A", "Reputational risk"], ["B", "Resource/supplier risk"], ["C", "Funding liquidity risk"], ["D", "Credit risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Production stoppage may eventually cause:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{financial}$$</div>\n<p>or:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{reputation}$$</div>\n<p>problems.</p>\n<p>But the original exposure is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>resource/supplier dependency</span></div>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 27, "question": "<p>A factory fire causes production to stop. Customers become angry, social-media criticism increases and sales decline.</p>\n<p>Which statement is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "The event can belong to multiple risk classifications depending on the dimension analysed"], ["B", "Every event must belong to exactly one risk category"], ["C", "The event is necessarily speculative risk"], ["D", "Because reputation is affected, the fire is not pure risk"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>This is an extremely important principle.</p>\n<p>The fire can simultaneously be:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Pure risk}$$</div>\n<p>under one classification,</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>operational/business exposure</span></div>\n<p>under another,</p>\n<p>and generate:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{reputational consequences}$$</div>\n<p>afterwards.</p>\n<p>Different classifications ask different questions.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 28, "question": "<p>An investor owns 200 technology companies. A government announces an economy-wide interest-rate increase.</p>\n<p>Why might diversification fail to eliminate the resulting risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "There are too many assets"], ["B", "The event affects many assets in the same direction"], ["C", "Technology firms have no unsystematic risk"], ["D", "Diversification increases systematic risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Diversification works best when company-specific events are imperfectly related.</p>\n<p>Systematic events affect many companies simultaneously.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 29, "question": "<p>Which is the best example of <strong>loss prevention rather than loss reduction</strong>?</p>", "options": [["A", "Fireproof walls limiting damage after a fire"], ["B", "Sprinklers suppressing a fire"], ["C", "Driver training reducing accident occurrence"], ["D", "Emergency funds helping a firm recover after disaster"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The wording:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>reducing accident occurrence</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>means:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Frequency}\\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>Therefore prevention.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 30, "question": "<p>A company sells goods on credit. One customer cannot pay. Because several other customers also delay payments, the company itself cannot meet payroll.</p>\n<p>Which sequence is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Market risk → systematic risk"], ["B", "Credit risk → funding liquidity risk"], ["C", "Asset liquidity risk → pure risk"], ["D", "Resource risk → market risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>First:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{customers do not repay}\n=\n\\boxed{\\text{Credit risk}}$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{company cannot meet short-term cash obligation}\n=\n\\boxed{\\text{Funding liquidity risk}}$$</div>\n<p>This is precisely how one risk can trigger another.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 1, "question": "<p>A business owner is personally responsible for all business debts.</p>\n<p>Which structure most clearly fits?</p>", "options": [["A", "Sdn. Bhd."], ["B", "Sole proprietorship"], ["C", "Bhd."], ["D", "LLP"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Sole proprietors have unlimited liability.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 2, "question": "<p>Which structure is described as a separate legal entity from its shareholders?</p>", "options": [["A", "Sole proprietorship"], ["B", "General partnership"], ["C", "Sdn. Bhd."], ["D", "Informal business"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides explicitly describe a Sdn. Bhd. as separate from its shareholders.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 3, "question": "<p>Which business form combines partnership-style flexibility with limited-liability protection?</p>", "options": [["A", "Sole proprietorship"], ["B", "LLP"], ["C", "General partnership"], ["D", "Bhd. only"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>LLP combines partnership characteristics with limited-liability protection.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 4, "question": "<p>Which statement best describes a corporation?</p>", "options": [["A", "Owners are always personally responsible for company obligations"], ["B", "The corporation is legally separate from owners"], ["C", "Ownership and management must always be identical"], ["D", "It cannot have a board"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The corporation is legally distinct from owners and is responsible for its own obligations.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 5, "question": "<p>Why can a sole proprietorship represent concentrated risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "Ownership, management and capital may depend heavily on one person"], ["B", "It always has too many shareholders"], ["C", "It has double taxation"], ["D", "It has no liability"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The same individual may provide ownership, management and capital, creating concentration.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 6, "question": "<p>A company decides whether to enter the Indonesian market.</p>\n<p>This is primarily:</p>", "options": [["A", "Operational risk"], ["B", "Strategic risk"], ["C", "Compliance risk only"], ["D", "Tactical risk only"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Entering a market changes the firm's long-term direction.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Strategic</span></div>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 7, "question": "<p>After deciding to enter Indonesia, management chooses how to finance the expansion.</p>\n<p>This is most closely associated with:</p>", "options": [["A", "Tactical risk"], ["B", "Pure risk"], ["C", "Operational risk only"], ["D", "Compliance risk only"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Financing is part of implementing the chosen strategy.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Tactical</span></div>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 8, "question": "<p>A factory's production system unexpectedly fails.</p>\n<p>This is:</p>", "options": [["A", "Strategic risk"], ["B", "Compliance risk"], ["C", "Operational risk"], ["D", "Tactical marketing risk"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Day-to-day system failure:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Operational</span></div>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 9, "question": "<p>A company fails to comply with required environmental legislation.</p>\n<p>This is:</p>", "options": [["A", "Compliance risk"], ["B", "Strategic risk"], ["C", "Credit risk"], ["D", "Tactical pricing risk"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Violation of laws/regulations:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Compliance</span></div>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 10, "question": "<p>Which statement is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Maximising sales always maximises profit"], ["B", "Minimising costs always increases long-term value"], ["C", "Corporate objectives can conflict"], ["D", "Shareholder wealth and current-year profit are always identical"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Corporate goals can conflict.</p>\n<p>Higher sales do not guarantee higher profit, for example.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 11, "question": "<p>Which sequence most closely reflects risk management in the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "Ignore → insure → recover"], ["B", "Identify → analyse → treat → monitor"], ["C", "Invest → borrow → insure → sell"], ["D", "Monitor → ignore → transfer"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter's logic is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Identify → Analyse → Treat → Monitor</span></div>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 12, "question": "<p>Which statement best describes risk management?</p>", "options": [["A", "It is only useful after a loss occurs"], ["B", "It is mainly a form of insurance"], ["C", "It is a logical and systematic approach supporting decision-making"], ["D", "Its only objective is to eliminate uncertainty"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Risk management is described as logical and systematic and as improving decision-making.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 13, "question": "<p>Buying appropriate insurance before a disaster is primarily associated with:</p>", "options": [["A", "Post-loss objective"], ["B", "Pre-loss objective"], ["C", "Strategic failure"], ["D", "Compliance failure"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Insurance is arranged before the loss.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Pre-loss</span></div>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 14, "question": "<p>Ensuring that the organisation survives following a major disaster is a:</p>", "options": [["A", "Pre-loss objective"], ["B", "Post-loss objective"], ["C", "Tactical objective only"], ["D", "Purely financial hedge"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Survival after an event is explicitly a post-loss objective.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 15, "question": "<p>Restoring business operations following a major loss is:</p>", "options": [["A", "Pre-loss"], ["B", "Post-loss"], ["C", "Avoidance"], ["D", "Strategic risk identification"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Continuity and recovery are post-loss objectives.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 16, "question": "<p>Historically, risk management was primarily:</p>", "options": [["A", "ESG-based"], ["B", "Insurance-based"], ["C", "AI-based"], ["D", "Strategic-value based"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Historically, the discipline was primarily insurance-based.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 17, "question": "<p>Traditional risk management mainly concentrated on:</p>", "options": [["A", "Physical hazards and financial risks"], ["B", "Sustainability and AI risks only"], ["C", "Strategic opportunities only"], ["D", "Enterprise-wide interdependencies"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Traditional RM focused particularly on physical hazards and financial risks.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 18, "question": "<p>Which is the defining weakness of silo risk management?</p>", "options": [["A", "It cannot insure physical assets"], ["B", "Risks may be assessed separately without recognising interactions"], ["C", "It considers too many risks together"], ["D", "It always overestimates strategic risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The core weakness is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>independent analysis may miss interdependencies</span></div>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 19, "question": "<p>Conventional corporate risk management mainly focuses on:</p>", "options": [["A", "Social and environmental risks only"], ["B", "Credit, market and liquidity risks"], ["C", "Reputation only"], ["D", "Employee welfare only"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Conventional CRM focuses strongly on:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>credit + market + liquidity</span></div>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 20, "question": "<p>Which tool is particularly associated with conventional financial risk management in the slides?</p>", "options": [["A", "Hedging"], ["B", "Employee volunteering"], ["C", "Corporate philanthropy"], ["D", "Sustainability reporting"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides mention hedging, including futures and options.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 21, "question": "<p>Which statement best describes ERM?</p>", "options": [["A", "Insurance for all company assets"], ["B", "Independent management of each departmental risk"], ["C", "A holistic framework for managing corporate risks to create long-term value"], ["D", "Elimination of all risk"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>ERM is holistic and value-oriented.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 22, "question": "<p>Which feature most clearly distinguishes ERM from silo risk management?</p>", "options": [["A", "ERM ignores financial risk"], ["B", "ERM assesses risks collectively across the enterprise"], ["C", "ERM removes the need for managers"], ["D", "ERM focuses only on insurance"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>ERM considers risks across the enterprise rather than independently.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 23, "question": "<p>A company separately manages FX, supply chain, credit and market risk. All four depend heavily on political stability in the same country, but nobody assesses the combined exposure.</p>\n<p>The main problem is:</p>", "options": [["A", "Too much diversification"], ["B", "Silo risk management"], ["C", "Excessive ERM"], ["D", "Post-loss management"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>This is a textbook silo problem.</p>\n<p>Every department may be technically correct in isolation while the enterprise-level conclusion is wrong.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 24, "question": "<p>Which statement is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "ERM tries to remove all uncertainty"], ["B", "ERM supports better decisions about risk and opportunity"], ["C", "ERM only deals with pure risk"], ["D", "ERM excludes strategy"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>ERM improves decision-making under uncertainty.</p>\n<p>It does not require zero risk.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 25, "question": "<p>Which approach is most clearly described as \"value-based\"?</p>", "options": [["A", "Pure insurance management"], ["B", "Enterprise Risk Management"], ["C", "Sole proprietorship management"], ["D", "Loss prevention only"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Your slides explicitly call ERM a:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>value-based management approach</span></div>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 26, "question": "<p>A company has four departments. Each reports that its risk is individually \"low.\" However, all risks could materialise simultaneously if oil prices collapse.</p>\n<p>What has management failed to consider?</p>", "options": [["A", "Limited liability"], ["B", "Risk interdependency and aggregation"], ["C", "Legal ownership"], ["D", "Post-loss survival"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>They failed to recognise:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>common drivers + risk correlation + aggregation</span></div>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 27, "question": "<p>Management has already approved an overseas acquisition. The risk department is brought in afterward only to insure the new assets.</p>\n<p>Which approach does this most closely resemble?</p>", "options": [["A", "Fully integrated ERM"], ["B", "Traditional/reactive risk management"], ["C", "Holistic sustainability risk management"], ["D", "Strategic risk integration"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Management decided first and only later transferred some financial consequences.</p>\n<p>That is much closer to traditional/reactive risk management than integrated ERM.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 28, "question": "<p>Before approving an acquisition, the board evaluates financial, strategic, operational, reputation and compliance risks together.</p>\n<p>This is most consistent with:</p>", "options": [["A", "Silo risk management"], ["B", "Enterprise Risk Management"], ["C", "Insurance-only RM"], ["D", "Pure-risk management"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Multiple enterprise risks are being considered collectively before the decision.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>ERM</span></div>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 29, "question": "<p>Which statement best captures the relationship between risk and corporate objectives?</p>", "options": [["A", "Risk management operates independently of strategy"], ["B", "Risk matters because uncertainty may affect organisational objectives"], ["C", "All corporate objectives can be maximised simultaneously"], ["D", "Only financial objectives create risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>This links directly back to Topic 1:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>risk matters because uncertainty affects objectives</span></div>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 30, "question": "<p>A company rejects every investment containing uncertainty.</p>\n<p>From an ERM perspective, this policy is problematic because:</p>", "options": [["A", "All risk must be accepted"], ["B", "Eliminating risk can also eliminate value-creating opportunities"], ["C", "ERM requires maximum risk-taking"], ["D", "Risk has no relationship to returns"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Business requires risk-taking.</p>\n<p>Eliminating every risky activity could also eliminate:</p>\n<p>innovation,</p>\n<p>growth,</p>\n<p>investment,</p>\n<p>opportunity.</p>\n<p>ERM seeks <strong>appropriate risk</strong>, not zero risk.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 31, "question": "<p>A company chooses to expand into Vietnam. Later, its accounting software fails and prevents invoices from being processed.</p>\n<p>Which statement is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Both are strategic risk"], ["B", "Expansion is strategic; software failure is operational"], ["C", "Expansion is operational; software failure is strategic"], ["D", "Both are tactical"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The <strong>decision direction</strong> is strategic.</p>\n<p>The <strong>execution failure</strong> is operational.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 32, "question": "<p>Management evaluates currency, supplier, reputation and regulatory risks before deciding whether to enter a country.</p>\n<p>What makes this closer to ERM than conventional CRM?</p>", "options": [["A", "It includes insurance"], ["B", "It considers a broad portfolio of risks as part of strategy"], ["C", "It considers currency risk"], ["D", "It uses financial information"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Currency risk by itself could easily belong to conventional CRM.</p>\n<p>What makes this ERM is the:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>breadth + integration + strategic connection</span></div>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 33, "question": "<p>After a major fire, a company wants to continue paying employees and serving customers.</p>\n<p>Which objective is most relevant?</p>", "options": [["A", "Pre-loss legal compliance"], ["B", "Post-loss continuity"], ["C", "Silo management"], ["D", "Tactical risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The loss has already happened.</p>\n<p>So the question concerns:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>continuity and survival</span></div>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 34, "question": "<p>A firm's risk department perfectly hedges foreign-exchange exposure but management launches a product that nobody wants.</p>\n<p>Which conclusion best supports ERM?</p>", "options": [["A", "Financial hedging removes all enterprise risk"], ["B", "Financial risks are the only significant risks"], ["C", "Strong financial-risk management does not eliminate strategic/business risk"], ["D", "The product failure is liquidity risk only"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>This is one of the reasons risk management broadened beyond financial risk.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 35, "question": "<p>Which statement best describes a \"holistic\" approach?</p>", "options": [["A", "Every department manages risk independently"], ["B", "The firm manages only the largest financial risk"], ["C", "Risks are considered together with strategy, governance and broader business functions"], ["D", "All risks are transferred to insurers"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": ""}, {"module": 3, "number": 1, "question": "<p>Which best describes corporate governance?</p>", "options": [["A", "Daily supervision of employees"], ["B", "A system through which an organisation is directed and controlled"], ["C", "Only financial auditing"], ["D", "Insurance management"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Corporate governance concerns how the organisation is directed and controlled.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 2, "question": "<p>Which activity is most clearly management rather than governance?</p>", "options": [["A", "Establishing board oversight"], ["B", "Defining accountability structures"], ["C", "Scheduling daily production"], ["D", "Reviewing governance policy"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Scheduling daily production is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Management</span></div>\n<p>not governance.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 3, "question": "<p>Which element of corporate governance concerns who must answer for decisions?</p>", "options": [["A", "Transparency"], ["B", "Accountability"], ["C", "Regulatory framework"], ["D", "Administrative structure"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Who is responsible?</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Accountability</span></div>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 4, "question": "<p>Which element is most closely associated with openness and disclosure?</p>", "options": [["A", "Accountability"], ["B", "Transparency"], ["C", "Ethics"], ["D", "Administration"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Openness and disclosure:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Transparency</span></div>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 5, "question": "<p>Which element addresses whether actions are morally appropriate rather than merely legal?</p>", "options": [["A", "Business ethics and social responsibility"], ["B", "Transparency only"], ["C", "Risk analytics"], ["D", "Portfolio management"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Ethics asks whether conduct is appropriate, not merely lawful.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 6, "question": "<p>Risk governance primarily ensures that:</p>", "options": [["A", "Every risk is insured"], ["B", "Risk-management and control frameworks are embedded in organisational culture, processes and structure"], ["C", "Only financial risks are managed"], ["D", "Managers eliminate uncertainty"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>That wording comes directly from the lecture's risk-governance idea.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 7, "question": "<p>Which best describes a risk-management framework?</p>", "options": [["A", "A single insurance contract"], ["B", "An organisation-specific structure for identifying, analysing, responding to and monitoring risk"], ["C", "A law criminalising fraud"], ["D", "An audit report only"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A framework structures the risk process across the organisation.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 8, "question": "<p>Which of the following is one of the seven risk-governance building blocks in the lecture?</p>", "options": [["A", "Portfolio management"], ["B", "Dividend management"], ["C", "Inventory accounting"], ["D", "Product pricing"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Portfolio management is one of the seven building blocks.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 9, "question": "<p>Which building block focuses on using analytical tools to understand risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "Stakeholder management"], ["B", "Risk analytics"], ["C", "Administrative structure"], ["D", "Risk transfer"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Analytical tools:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk Analytics</span></div>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 10, "question": "<p>Which building block focuses on improving transparency for key stakeholders?</p>", "options": [["A", "Stakeholder management"], ["B", "Risk transfer"], ["C", "Line management"], ["D", "Portfolio management"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Transparency to stakeholders:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Stakeholder Management</span></div>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 11, "question": "<p>GRC stands for:</p>", "options": [["A", "Governance, Reporting and Capital"], ["B", "Governance, Risk Management and Compliance"], ["C", "Growth, Risk and Control"], ["D", "Governance, Regulation and Credit"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nGRC = Governance + Risk Management + Compliance\n}$$</div>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 12, "question": "<p>Which is the most appropriate role of the board?</p>", "options": [["A", "Perform every operational task"], ["B", "Oversee major risks and ensure effective governance/control structures"], ["C", "Replace line managers"], ["D", "Run every production shift"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Board:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>oversight</span></div>\n<p>Management:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>execution</span></div>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 13, "question": "<p>Which statement is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Management and governance are identical"], ["B", "Governance provides oversight while management performs daily operations"], ["C", "Governance only applies after losses"], ["D", "Management is responsible for external regulation"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>That's the core management-governance distinction.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 14, "question": "<p>SOX was enacted largely in response to:</p>", "options": [["A", "Climate-change regulation"], ["B", "Enron and WorldCom scandals"], ["C", "Asian financial crisis only"], ["D", "COVID-19"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>SOX followed high-profile fraud cases including Enron and WorldCom.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 15, "question": "<p>Which is a core objective of SOX?</p>", "options": [["A", "Promote tourism"], ["B", "Improve accuracy and reliability of corporate disclosures"], ["C", "Eliminate strategic risk"], ["D", "Replace auditors"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The lecture explicitly emphasises improving corporate-disclosure accuracy and reliability.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 16, "question": "<p>SOX primarily strengthens:</p>", "options": [["A", "Corporate controls, disclosures and accountability"], ["B", "Environmental management only"], ["C", "Insurance claims"], ["D", "Supply-chain diversification"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>SOX is strongly associated with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>controls + disclosure + accountability</span></div>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 17, "question": "<p>Which statement best distinguishes SOX from ISO 31000?</p>", "options": [["A", "SOX is a law/regulatory requirement; ISO 31000 is risk-management guidance/standard"], ["B", "Both are identical laws"], ["C", "ISO 31000 applies only to accounting fraud"], ["D", "SOX is a voluntary risk vocabulary"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>This is the key distinction.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 18, "question": "<p>Which Malaysian body is most closely associated with the Malaysian Code on Corporate Governance?</p>", "options": [["A", "Securities Commission"], ["B", "Bank Negara only"], ["C", "SSM only"], ["D", "Inland Revenue Board"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Securities Commission → MCCG\"><span>Securities Commission</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>MCCG</span></div>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 19, "question": "<p>Which body provides risk-governance guidance particularly relevant to financial institutions?</p>", "options": [["A", "Bank Negara Malaysia"], ["B", "Bursa Malaysia only"], ["C", "PCG"], ["D", "ASX"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"BNM → Financial Institutions\"><span>BNM</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Financial Institutions</span></div>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 20, "question": "<p>The Green Book is associated in the lecture with:</p>", "options": [["A", "Public listed companies generally"], ["B", "Government-linked companies"], ["C", "Sole proprietorships"], ["D", "Foreign banks only"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Green Book → GLCs\"><span>Green Book</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>GLCs</span></div>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 21, "question": "<p>The MCCG asks companies to:</p>", "options": [["A", "Ignore principal risks"], ["B", "Identify principal risks and implement appropriate systems to manage them"], ["C", "Insure all strategic risk"], ["D", "Avoid all uncertainty"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>This mirrors the lecture wording.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 22, "question": "<p>COSO ERM is presented in the lecture using which three dimensions?</p>", "options": [["A", "Profit, people, planet"], ["B", "Objectives, ERM components, organisational levels"], ["C", "Risk, return, liquidity"], ["D", "Strategy, tax, audit"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>COSO:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Objectives}\n+\n\\text{ERM Components}\n+\n\\text{Organisation Levels}\n}$$</div>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 23, "question": "<p>Which COSO component deals with organisational culture and risk attitude?</p>", "options": [["A", "Internal environment"], ["B", "Risk response"], ["C", "Monitoring"], ["D", "Control activity"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Culture/tone:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Internal Environment</span></div>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 24, "question": "<p>Which COSO component asks \"What events could affect our objectives?\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Objective setting"], ["B", "Event identification"], ["C", "Monitoring"], ["D", "Risk response"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>\"What might happen?\"</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Event Identification</span></div>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 25, "question": "<p>Which COSO component evaluates likelihood and impact?</p>", "options": [["A", "Risk assessment"], ["B", "Control activity"], ["C", "Information and communication"], ["D", "Internal environment"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Likelihood and impact:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk Assessment</span></div>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 26, "question": "<p>Which COSO component concerns what management will do about a risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "Event identification"], ["B", "Risk response"], ["C", "Monitoring"], ["D", "Objective setting"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>\"What do we do?\"</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk Response</span></div>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 27, "question": "<p>Dual authorisation for major payments is an example of:</p>", "options": [["A", "Objective setting"], ["B", "Control activity"], ["C", "Monitoring only"], ["D", "Event identification"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Dual authorisation is a specific control.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 28, "question": "<p>Ensuring risk information reaches the board relates most closely to:</p>", "options": [["A", "Information and communication"], ["B", "Risk response"], ["C", "Internal environment"], ["D", "Objective setting"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Risk information reaching decision-makers:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Information and Communication</span></div>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 29, "question": "<p>ISO 31000 is structured around:</p>", "options": [["A", "Principles, framework and process"], ["B", "Fraud, audit and tax"], ["C", "Governance, compliance and finance"], ["D", "Insurance, hedging and credit"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>ISO 31000:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Principles + Framework + Process</span></div>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 30, "question": "<p>Which is an ISO 31000 principle in the lecture?</p>", "options": [["A", "Risk management should be separate from decision-making"], ["B", "Risk management should create value"], ["C", "Risk management should ignore human factors"], ["D", "Risk management should be static"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The lecture explicitly says risk management should create value.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 31, "question": "<p>Which statement best reflects ISO 31000?</p>", "options": [["A", "It should be tailored to the organisation"], ["B", "It should be identical for every organisation"], ["C", "It excludes uncertainty"], ["D", "It is unrelated to decision-making"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>ISO says risk management should be tailored.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 32, "question": "<p>Which comes first in the ISO framework shown in the lecture?</p>", "options": [["A", "Continual improvement"], ["B", "Mandate and commitment"], ["C", "Monitoring"], ["D", "Risk transfer"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Mandate and Commitment</span></div>\n<p>comes first in the lecture's ISO framework.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 33, "question": "<p>ISO/IEC 31010 primarily provides:</p>", "options": [["A", "Corporate-law penalties"], ["B", "Risk-assessment techniques"], ["C", "Accounting standards"], ["D", "Board structures"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nISO/IEC\\ 31010 = Risk\\ Assessment\\ Techniques\n}$$</div>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 34, "question": "<p>ISO Guide 73 provides:</p>", "options": [["A", "Risk-management vocabulary"], ["B", "Financial penalties"], ["C", "Strategic goals"], ["D", "Audit certification"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nISO\\ Guide\\ 73 = Risk\\ Management\\ Vocabulary\n}$$</div>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 35, "question": "<p>Which step comes before identifying risk in the AS/NZS process?</p>", "options": [["A", "Establish the context"], ["B", "Monitor and review"], ["C", "Risk treatment"], ["D", "Insurance transfer"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Establish the Context</span></div>\n<p>comes before identifying risk.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 36, "question": "<p>Which is part of the AS/NZS process?</p>", "options": [["A", "Communicate and consult"], ["B", "Ignore stakeholders"], ["C", "Eliminate all uncertainty"], ["D", "Avoid objective setting"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Communication and consultation are explicitly part of the process.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 37, "question": "<p>The AS/NZS framework aims to promote:</p>", "options": [["A", "Reactive rather than proactive management"], ["B", "Better decision-making and proactive risk management"], ["C", "Purely financial control"], ["D", "Only insurance management"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The lecture emphasises proactive management and better decision-making.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 38, "question": "<p>A company has an excellent written risk policy, but executives routinely ignore it and the board never challenges management.</p>\n<p>What is the best diagnosis?</p>", "options": [["A", "Strong risk governance because a policy exists"], ["B", "Weak risk governance because the framework is not embedded in behaviour and oversight"], ["C", "Strong compliance because documentation exists"], ["D", "Pure operational risk only"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>This is exactly what \"embedded in culture, processes and structure\" means.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 39, "question": "<p>A finance director can create a vendor, approve the payment and reconcile the transaction alone.</p>\n<p>What is the most immediate weakness?</p>", "options": [["A", "Strategic risk"], ["B", "Internal-control weakness"], ["C", "Environmental risk"], ["D", "Portfolio management problem"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The issue is inadequate segregation of duties.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 40, "question": "<p>The board receives monthly reports, but the reports intentionally exclude major project overruns.</p>\n<p>Which governance element is weakest?</p>", "options": [["A", "Transparency"], ["B", "Risk transfer"], ["C", "Portfolio management"], ["D", "Market risk"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The core problem is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>information is being withheld</span></div>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 41, "question": "<p>A firm complies with every applicable reporting rule but approves a disastrous acquisition without analysing strategic risk.</p>\n<p>Which statement is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Compliance guarantees strong ERM"], ["B", "Strong compliance can coexist with poor risk management"], ["C", "The acquisition cannot fail because the company complied with law"], ["D", "Compliance eliminates strategic risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>This distinction is very important.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 42, "question": "<p>A company introduces a dual-signature rule for large payments. The board periodically reviews whether the rule is still effective.</p>\n<p>Which pairing is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "Dual signature = governance; board review = operational risk"], ["B", "Dual signature = control activity; board review = oversight/monitoring"], ["C", "Both are event identification"], ["D", "Both are risk transfer"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": ""}, {"module": 3, "number": 43, "question": "<p>Management defines a growth target first, then identifies events that could prevent the target from being achieved.</p>\n<p>Which COSO sequence is being followed?</p>", "options": [["A", "Risk response → monitoring"], ["B", "Objective setting → event identification"], ["C", "Control activities → internal environment"], ["D", "Monitoring → event identification"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": ""}, {"module": 3, "number": 44, "question": "<p>A firm copies another company's entire risk framework even though its own business model is completely different.</p>\n<p>Which ISO principle is most clearly violated?</p>", "options": [["A", "Tailored"], ["B", "Transparent"], ["C", "Creates value"], ["D", "Continual improvement"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Risk management should fit the organisation.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 45, "question": "<p>A company relies on outdated risk data even though better information is available.</p>\n<p>Which ISO principle is most directly violated?</p>", "options": [["A", "Based on the best available information"], ["B", "Tailored"], ["C", "Human factors"], ["D", "Risk transfer"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": ""}, {"module": 3, "number": 46, "question": "<p>A company updates its risk framework every year as technology, markets and regulation change.</p>\n<p>Which ISO principle is best reflected?</p>", "options": [["A", "Static management"], ["B", "Dynamic, iterative and responsive to change"], ["C", "Risk elimination"], ["D", "Pure compliance"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": ""}, {"module": 4, "number": 1, "question": "<p>Which statement best represents sustainability?</p>", "options": [["A", "Maximising present economic output regardless of future effects"], ["B", "Protecting nature by completely stopping economic activity"], ["C", "Meeting current needs without compromising future generations' needs"], ["D", "Maximising shareholder wealth through environmental marketing"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Meeting current needs without compromising future generations' needs</strong></p>\n<p>The defining feature is intergenerational responsibility combined with economic, social and environmental balance.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 2, "question": "<p>A company generates high profit by exhausting a non-renewable resource without planning for future productive capacity. What is the main sustainability concern?</p>", "options": [["A", "The company is not profitable"], ["B", "Current performance undermines future capacity"], ["C", "The company is necessarily violating accounting standards"], ["D", "Any use of natural resources is prohibited"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Current performance undermines future capacity</strong></p>\n<p>Sustainability asks whether present activities compromise future needs, not whether current profit is positive.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 3, "question": "<p>Which three dimensions make up the Triple Bottom Line?</p>", "options": [["A", "Policy, People, Prosperity"], ["B", "Profit, Peace, Partnership"], ["C", "People, Planet, Peace"], ["D", "People, Planet, Profit"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>People, Planet, Profit</strong></p>\n<p>The Triple Bottom Line combines social, environmental and economic dimensions.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 4, "question": "<p>Which is NOT one of the five overarching themes of the UN 2030 Agenda?</p>", "options": [["A", "Performance"], ["B", "People"], ["C", "Peace"], ["D", "Partnership"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Performance</strong></p>\n<p>The 5Ps are People, Planet, Prosperity, Peace and Partnership.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 5, "question": "<p>Which historical milestone is most closely associated with defining sustainable development in relation to the needs of future generations?</p>", "options": [["A", "The 2004 Kofi Annan letter"], ["B", "The launch of FTSE4Good Bursa Malaysia"], ["C", "The 1987 Brundtland Commission report"], ["D", "The 2015 adoption of the SDGs"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>The 1987 Brundtland Commission report</strong></p>\n<p>The Brundtland Commission is the central historical starting point in your lecture.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 6, "question": "<p>How many Millennium Development Goals preceded the SDGs?</p>", "options": [["A", "5"], ["B", "8"], ["C", "17"], ["D", "169"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>8</strong></p>\n<p>The MDGs contained eight goals, compared with 17 SDGs and 169 targets.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 7, "question": "<p>Which combination correctly describes the UN Sustainable Development Goals?</p>", "options": [["A", "8 goals, 169 targets"], ["B", "17 goals, 17 targets"], ["C", "5 goals, 169 targets"], ["D", "17 goals, 169 targets"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>17 goals, 169 targets</strong></p>\n<p>The 2015 SDG agenda contains 17 goals and 169 targets.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 8, "question": "<p>Which statement most accurately distinguishes the SDGs from ESG?</p>", "options": [["A", "SDGs and ESG are alternative names for the same rating system"], ["B", "SDGs are global goals; ESG concerns organisational environmental, social and governance factors"], ["C", "ESG contains 17 global goals, while SDGs contain three corporate ratings"], ["D", "SDGs apply only to government and cannot involve companies"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>SDGs are global goals; ESG concerns organisational environmental, social and governance factors</strong></p>\n<p>The SDGs specify global development ambitions; ESG is an organisational and investment-related lens.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 9, "question": "<p>A company embeds workforce welfare, resource conservation and long-term financial viability into business strategy. This best demonstrates:</p>", "options": [["A", "Corporate sustainability"], ["B", "Greenwashing"], ["C", "Financial reporting alone"], ["D", "Pure regulatory compliance"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Corporate sustainability</strong></p>\n<p>Corporate sustainability incorporates sustainability into business strategy and activities.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 10, "question": "<p>Which initiative would most clearly represent substantive sustainability improvement rather than a purely promotional CSR activity?</p>", "options": [["A", "Replacing the annual report cover with a forest photograph"], ["B", "Issuing a new sustainability slogan"], ["C", "Redesigning supplier practices to reduce verified labour and environmental impacts"], ["D", "Donating a small amount while refusing to assess material operating risks"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Redesigning supplier practices to reduce verified labour and environmental impacts</strong></p>\n<p>A real operational change addressing material impacts is stronger evidence of corporate sustainability than branding alone.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 11, "question": "<p>A plantation company's operations result in substantial deforestation. Which ESG dimension is most directly implicated?</p>", "options": [["A", "Governance"], ["B", "Social"], ["C", "Environmental"], ["D", "Financial accounting"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Environmental</strong></p>\n<p>Deforestation and biodiversity are environmental factors.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 12, "question": "<p>An electronics manufacturer is accused of requiring excessive overtime and unsafe working conditions. The primary ESG dimension is:</p>", "options": [["A", "Social"], ["B", "Environmental"], ["C", "Governance only"], ["D", "Market risk only"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Social</strong></p>\n<p>Labour conditions and worker health and safety are Social factors.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 13, "question": "<p>Shareholders question unusually high executive remuneration despite weak performance. Which ESG dimension is most directly involved?</p>", "options": [["A", "Environmental"], ["B", "Governance"], ["C", "Social only"], ["D", "Climate risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Governance</strong></p>\n<p>Executive compensation and board accountability fall under Governance.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 14, "question": "<p>A technology company mishandles customers' personal information. Under the chapter's ESG classification, this is most directly associated with:</p>", "options": [["A", "Deforestation"], ["B", "Energy consumption"], ["C", "Executive remuneration"], ["D", "Social issues involving privacy and data protection"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Social issues involving privacy and data protection</strong></p>\n<p>The Social slide explicitly includes privacy and data protection.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 15, "question": "<p>A factory pollutes a river, and directors intentionally conceal the event from regulators. Which analysis is strongest?</p>", "options": [["A", "Only Social risk exists"], ["B", "The problem is exclusively financial"], ["C", "Environmental harm is compounded by governance failure"], ["D", "There is no ESG issue unless the share price declines"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Environmental harm is compounded by governance failure</strong></p>\n<p>Pollution is Environmental; deliberate concealment adds Governance risk.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 16, "question": "<p>A company's factory is flooded during extreme weather. The flood interrupts production and reduces revenue. Which chain best describes the situation?</p>", "options": [["A", "Governance → advertising → reputation"], ["B", "Credit risk → stock valuation"], ["C", "Employee diversity → market liquidity"], ["D", "Environmental hazard → operational disruption → financial consequence"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Environmental hazard → operational disruption → financial consequence</strong></p>\n<p>An environmental hazard can transmit into operational and financial risk.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 17, "question": "<p>Which factor should be particularly important in deciding which ESG risks a company prioritises?</p>", "options": [["A", "How popular the topic is on social media"], ["B", "Materiality to the company's activities and objectives"], ["C", "Whether competitors use the same sustainability slogans"], ["D", "Whether the issue appears first in an ESG checklist"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Materiality to the company's activities and objectives</strong></p>\n<p>Materiality directs attention to ESG issues capable of significantly affecting operations, objectives or value.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 18, "question": "<p>Two firms have identical environmental exposures. Company A has effective monitoring and controls, while Company B has weak controls. Which statement is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Their unmanaged risk may differ despite similar exposure"], ["B", "Their ESG risks must be identical"], ["C", "Company A has zero risk"], ["D", "Company B necessarily has better sustainability performance"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Their unmanaged risk may differ despite similar exposure</strong></p>\n<p>Exposure is only part of the risk picture; management effectiveness matters too.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 19, "question": "<p>A listed company improves its ESG rating without meaningful evidence that its underlying operations improved. What is the best interpretation?</p>", "options": [["A", "Its sustainability is proven"], ["B", "Its financial returns must rise"], ["C", "Its operational risks have disappeared"], ["D", "A better rating alone is insufficient evidence of substantive ESG improvement"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>A better rating alone is insufficient evidence of substantive ESG improvement</strong></p>\n<p>ESG ratings are indicators, not identical to sustainability performance.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 20, "question": "<p>A Malaysian manufacturer's products face import restrictions after allegations of forced labour involving its operations or supply chain. Which sequence is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Governance → interest-rate risk"], ["B", "Environmental pollution → insurance claims"], ["C", "Social risk → market access and revenue consequences"], ["D", "Pure financial risk without ESG relevance"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Social risk → market access and revenue consequences</strong></p>\n<p>Labour practices are Social; trade restrictions can produce business and financial consequences.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 21, "question": "<p>Which statement most clearly describes greenwashing?</p>", "options": [["A", "Promoting unsupported or misleading environmental credentials"], ["B", "Publishing an accurate report showing poor environmental performance"], ["C", "Reducing carbon emissions through verified efficiency improvements"], ["D", "Disclosing material environmental liabilities"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Promoting unsupported or misleading environmental credentials</strong></p>\n<p>Greenwashing concerns misleading claims or impressions, not poor performance by itself.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 22, "question": "<p>A company publicly admits that emissions increased and supplies transparent, verified evidence. What is the most accurate conclusion?</p>", "options": [["A", "It is automatically greenwashing"], ["B", "Poor environmental performance alone does not prove greenwashing"], ["C", "It has achieved sustainable development"], ["D", "Its ESG rating must improve"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Poor environmental performance alone does not prove greenwashing</strong></p>\n<p>Truthful disclosure of weak results is different from misleading sustainability communication.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 23, "question": "<p>A company claims all products are carbon-neutral but has no credible evidence supporting that claim. Which is the most immediate concern?</p>", "options": [["A", "Credit risk only"], ["B", "A proven reduction in environmental risk"], ["C", "Greenwashing and disclosure credibility"], ["D", "A guaranteed increase in enterprise value"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Greenwashing and disclosure credibility</strong></p>\n<p>Unsupported environmental claims create greenwashing and governance/reputational exposure.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 24, "question": "<p>Management promises a 50% reduction in greenhouse-gas emissions but cannot determine its existing emissions. What should be prioritised?</p>", "options": [["A", "Announcing a more ambitious target"], ["B", "Publishing a promotional campaign"], ["C", "Applying for ESG index inclusion"], ["D", "Establishing a reliable emissions baseline"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Establishing a reliable emissions baseline</strong></p>\n<p>Without a reliable baseline, progress against the reduction target cannot be measured credibly.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 25, "question": "<p>Which distinction between ESG reporting and ESG risk management is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Reporting communicates ESG information; risk management identifies, assesses and responds to ESG risks"], ["B", "Reporting automatically eliminates operational risk"], ["C", "Risk management is mainly concerned with choosing report colours"], ["D", "The two activities are unrelated"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Reporting communicates ESG information; risk management identifies, assesses and responds to ESG risks</strong></p>\n<p>Good reporting supports transparency, while risk management involves substantive identification and response.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 26, "question": "<p>Which pairing is most appropriate?</p>", "options": [["A", "GRI → US corporate fraud legislation"], ["B", "GRI → sustainability impact reporting; ISSB → sustainability-related financial disclosures"], ["C", "ISSB → only charitable donations"], ["D", "ISO → FTSE4Good constituent selection"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>GRI → sustainability impact reporting; ISSB → sustainability-related financial disclosures</strong></p>\n<p>GRI and ISSB address different reporting perspectives, rather than being interchangeable instruments.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 27, "question": "<p>Which organisation introduced international sustainability-disclosure standards discussed in the lecture?</p>", "options": [["A", "World Trade Organization"], ["B", "Bank Negara Malaysia alone"], ["C", "International Sustainability Standards Board"], ["D", "FTSE Russell exclusively"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>International Sustainability Standards Board</strong></p>\n<p>The ISSB is highlighted in the lecture's sustainability-reporting standards discussion.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 28, "question": "<p>A company issues financing to fund an eligible renewable-energy project, while an investment fund purchases shares in environmentally oriented businesses. Which distinction is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Project financing illustrates green finance; the fund's allocation illustrates green investing"], ["B", "Both activities are necessarily greenwashing"], ["C", "Only the share purchase is green finance"], ["D", "Neither involves environmental considerations"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Project financing illustrates green finance; the fund's allocation illustrates green investing</strong></p>\n<p>Green finance focuses on financing green activities; green investing takes the investor's allocation perspective.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 29, "question": "<p>A manufacturer maps its emissions-reduction programme to SDG 13. Which statement is the most appropriate?</p>", "options": [["A", "It proves the company has achieved all 17 SDGs"], ["B", "It guarantees FTSE4Good inclusion"], ["C", "A single SDG mapping establishes sustainability across the business"], ["D", "A relevant ESG initiative can contribute to an SDG, but the claimed contribution still requires evidence"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>A relevant ESG initiative can contribute to an SDG, but the claimed contribution still requires evidence</strong></p>\n<p>SDG alignment can show relevance, but labels alone do not verify results.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 30, "question": "<p>What does net-zero greenhouse-gas emissions mean in the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "No company may produce any emissions under any circumstances"], ["B", "Emissions produced are balanced by greenhouse-gas removals"], ["C", "Emissions must grow at the same rate as profit"], ["D", "Carbon reporting becomes unnecessary"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Emissions produced are balanced by greenhouse-gas removals</strong></p>\n<p>Net zero concerns a balance between emissions produced and removals, not necessarily zero gross emissions.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 31, "question": "<p>According to the lecture, FTSE4Good Bursa Malaysia was launched in December 2014 and draws its constituents from:</p>", "options": [["A", "All Malaysian private businesses"], ["B", "Only Malaysian government-linked banks"], ["C", "The FTSE Bursa Malaysia EMAS index universe"], ["D", "Only companies issuing green sukuk"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>The FTSE Bursa Malaysia EMAS index universe</strong></p>\n<p>The lecture identifies the FBM EMAS index as the constituent universe and December 2014 as the launch date.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 32, "question": "<p>A manufacturer wants FTSE4Good inclusion. Which strategy is most consistent with meaningful ESG risk management?</p>", "options": [["A", "Focus entirely on publicity"], ["B", "Purchase more ESG advertising"], ["C", "Ignore suppliers and concentrate on the index score"], ["D", "Identify material ESG exposures, improve controls, measure outcomes and then pursue index eligibility"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Identify material ESG exposures, improve controls, measure outcomes and then pursue index eligibility</strong></p>\n<p>Index inclusion should follow credible performance, not substitute for it.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 33, "question": "<p>Which PETRONAS-related ESG issue does the lecture highlight for 2021?</p>", "options": [["A", "Exclusion from certain JPMorgan ESG-related indices"], ["B", "A major US food-safety prosecution"], ["C", "An employee stock-option controversy only"], ["D", "A ban on all PETRONAS products"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Exclusion from certain JPMorgan ESG-related indices</strong></p>\n<p>The lecture uses PETRONAS as an example of ESG-index exclusion. It does not establish the other choices.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 34, "question": "<p>Which underlying ESG factor is central to the Malaysian glove and palm-oil cases involving US import restrictions?</p>", "options": [["A", "Board gender diversity"], ["B", "Forced-labour allegations"], ["C", "Water pricing only"], ["D", "Corporate dividend policy"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Forced-labour allegations</strong></p>\n<p>The slides connect these restrictions with labour-related allegations, an S factor.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 35, "question": "<p>The Sapura Energy example primarily highlights concerns about:</p>", "options": [["A", "Biodiversity"], ["B", "Pollution"], ["C", "Executive remuneration and governance"], ["D", "Renewable electricity generation"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Executive remuneration and governance</strong></p>\n<p>The lecture links the case with objections to excessive executive compensation.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 36, "question": "<p>The Serba Dinamik case in the lecture most directly illustrates ESG concerns involving:</p>", "options": [["A", "Climate adaptation"], ["B", "Employee healthcare access"], ["C", "Water scarcity"], ["D", "Financial-reporting integrity and governance"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Financial-reporting integrity and governance</strong></p>\n<p>Financial-statement issues concern the reliability of reporting and company governance.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 37, "question": "<p>The Tesla ESG-index exclusion example in the lecture highlights concerns involving working conditions, discrimination and safety-related investigations. Which is the best interpretation?</p>", "options": [["A", "Social and governance concerns can affect ESG-index decisions even for a company selling electric vehicles"], ["B", "Electric vehicles automatically guarantee high ESG ratings"], ["C", "Environmental products eliminate all Social risks"], ["D", "Only carbon emissions matter in an ESG index"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Social and governance concerns can affect ESG-index decisions even for a company selling electric vehicles</strong></p>\n<p>An environmentally oriented product does not eliminate Social or Governance concerns.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 38, "question": "<p>The Tlou Energy case is used in the lecture to illustrate:</p>", "options": [["A", "Successful elimination of all emissions"], ["B", "Regulatory consequences of incorrect environmental claims"], ["C", "Only conventional credit risk"], ["D", "Compulsory FTSE4Good membership"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Regulatory consequences of incorrect environmental claims</strong></p>\n<p>The case involves Australian regulatory action concerning incorrect environmental claims.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 39, "question": "<p>The Coca-Cola case in the lecture primarily raises questions about:</p>", "options": [["A", "How to calculate bond duration"], ["B", "The legality of dividend payments"], ["C", "Whether environmental marketing claims are consistent with plastic-waste impacts"], ["D", "Bank Negara's monetary policy"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Whether environmental marketing claims are consistent with plastic-waste impacts</strong></p>\n<p>The slide uses allegations and criticism surrounding sustainability marketing and plastic waste to illustrate greenwashing risk.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 40, "question": "<p>A factory has repeated near-miss safety incidents but has not yet experienced a serious accident. Which would be the most appropriate ESG early-warning indicator?</p>", "options": [["A", "Number of trees in its annual report"], ["B", "Total market capitalisation alone"], ["C", "Number of sustainability slogans"], ["D", "Near-miss frequency and severity trends"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Near-miss frequency and severity trends</strong></p>\n<p>Near-miss trends can act as operational Social-risk warning indicators before a severe incident.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 41, "question": "<p>A manufacturing board has limited ESG resources. Which action should come first?</p>", "options": [["A", "Identify its material ESG exposures and objectives"], ["B", "Publish the longest possible report"], ["C", "Copy all ESG targets used by an unrelated bank"], ["D", "Allocate every ESG issue the same priority"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Identify its material ESG exposures and objectives</strong></p>\n<p>Materiality and objectives guide which issues deserve resources and controls.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 42, "question": "<p>A company lacks supplier assessments, emissions data and board oversight but wants sustainability financing. Which sequence is most credible?</p>", "options": [["A", "Apply for financing → announce success → consider data later"], ["B", "Establish oversight → identify material risks → collect baselines → introduce controls → report outcomes"], ["C", "Buy an ESG rating → declare compliance → avoid supplier assessment"], ["D", "Publish a glossy report → assume risk management is complete"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Establish oversight → identify material risks → collect baselines → introduce controls → report outcomes</strong></p>\n<p>Substantive governance, measurement and operational action should precede claims of ESG achievement.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 43, "question": "<p>A CEO's bonus depends only on short-term profit, even though safety and environmental incidents are increasing. What is the strongest diagnosis?</p>", "options": [["A", "Financial incentives guarantee sustainable performance"], ["B", "The problem is only with the marketing department"], ["C", "Governance incentives may undermine environmental and social objectives"], ["D", "There is no issue because profit is increasing"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Governance incentives may undermine environmental and social objectives</strong></p>\n<p>Incentive design is a Governance concern and can directly influence E and S outcomes.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 44, "question": "<p>An industrial company's emissions harm surrounding communities. New regulation then raises its operating costs. Which interpretation best reflects ESG risk management?</p>", "options": [["A", "Only environmental impact matters; financial consequences are irrelevant"], ["B", "ESG exists solely to maximise ratings"], ["C", "Regulatory costs prove that sustainability is unrelated to enterprise value"], ["D", "The company's environmental impact and the resulting financial exposure are interconnected"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>The company's environmental impact and the resulting financial exposure are interconnected</strong></p>\n<p>An outward environmental impact can generate inward operational, regulatory and financial risk.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 45, "question": "<p>A company's verified environmental investment costs RM20 million and is expected to reduce operating costs, regulatory exposure and future disruption. Which approach is most consistent with ERM?</p>", "options": [["A", "Evaluate the investment's costs, expected benefits, uncertainty and long-term enterprise value"], ["B", "Reject it because sustainability costs money"], ["C", "Approve it solely to generate positive publicity"], ["D", "Assume every green investment produces a guaranteed financial return"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p><strong>Evaluate the investment's costs, expected benefits, uncertainty and long-term enterprise value</strong></p>\n<p>ERM evaluates the trade-off between costs, risk reduction, opportunities and long-term value, rather than blindly accepting or rejecting a project.</p>"}];
window.DIFFICULT_BANK = [{"module": 1, "number": "D1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which wording matches the broader (ISO 31000) definition of risk used in the slides?</p>", "options": [["A", "The effect of uncertainty on objectives"], ["B", "The variability in expected return on an investment"], ["C", "The likelihood of experiencing harm or losing something of value"], ["D", "A situation in which a loss is possible regardless of whether it occurs"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>ISO 31000 defines risk as the <strong>effect of uncertainty on objectives</strong>, where the effect may be positive, negative or a deviation. C is the traditional definition, B the finance definition, and D defines a <strong>loss exposure</strong>, not risk.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>\"The probable size of the losses that may occur\" defines:</p>", "options": [["A", "Loss frequency"], ["B", "Loss severity"], ["C", "Loss exposure"], ["D", "Expected loss"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Severity is the <strong>size</strong> of losses. Frequency is the probable <strong>number</strong> of losses in a period. Exposure is the situation in which loss is possible, and expected loss combines frequency and severity.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which set lists only synonyms the slides use for <strong>diversifiable</strong> risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "Pure, particular, speculative"], ["B", "Systematic, fundamental, market risk"], ["C", "Non-systematic, particular, firm-specific"], ["D", "Non-systematic, fundamental, firm-specific"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Diversifiable risk is also called <strong>non-systematic, particular or firm-specific</strong> risk. \"Systematic\" and \"fundamental\" describe non-diversifiable risk, so B and D mix the two sides; A mixes in outcome-structure terms.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why can an unexpectedly <strong>good</strong> outcome still be a concern for risk management under the broader definition?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because risk management only measures negative deviations"], ["B", "Because positive outcomes are pure risks"], ["C", "Because good outcomes are always followed by losses"], ["D", "Because any deviation from objectives, including a positive one, reflects uncertainty that can create new problems such as capacity shortages"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Risk is the effect of uncertainty on objectives, positive or negative. A demand surge can cause stock-outs, quality problems and supplier strain. A contradicts the broader definition; C is not a rule; B misuses \"pure risk\", which has no gain possibility.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>What distinguishes risk from uncertainty in the slides?</p>", "options": [["A", "Risk involves losses; uncertainty involves gains"], ["B", "Risk applies to firms; uncertainty applies to individuals"], ["C", "Under risk, outcomes are known and odds may be known; under uncertainty, even the outcomes may not be known"], ["D", "Risk is always insurable; uncertainty is never insurable"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The distinction is about <strong>information</strong>: under risk the outcome set is known and probabilities may be estimable; under uncertainty the outcomes themselves may be unknown. The other options invent distinctions not in the slides.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why does the market compensate investors for systematic risk but not for unsystematic risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "Unsystematic risk can be removed cheaply through diversification, so bearing it is a choice the market does not reward"], ["B", "Systematic risk is always larger"], ["C", "Unsystematic risk cannot be measured"], ["D", "Regulators prohibit pricing unsystematic risk"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Investors could diversify firm-specific risk away themselves, so the market prices only the risk they cannot avoid: systematic risk, measured by beta. B is not necessarily true; C and D are false.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Beta is defined as Cov(Ri, Rm) / Var(Rm). Why can a highly volatile stock still have a low beta?</p>", "options": [["A", "Beta measures co-movement with the market; a stock can swing a lot for firm-specific reasons while barely moving with the market"], ["B", "Volatility and beta are the same thing"], ["C", "Low beta means low total risk"], ["D", "Beta only applies to bonds"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Beta captures systematic sensitivity. Large idiosyncratic swings raise total volatility without raising covariance with the market.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A warehouse installs automatic sprinklers and fire doors. Which technique, and which dimension does it target?</p>", "options": [["A", "Loss prevention; frequency"], ["B", "Loss reduction; frequency"], ["C", "Avoidance; frequency"], ["D", "Loss reduction; severity"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Sprinklers and fire doors limit damage <strong>once a fire has started</strong>, so they reduce <strong>severity</strong>. They do not stop fires starting (that would be prevention), and the exposure is retained, so it is not avoidance.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A bank lends RM500,000 to a developer. Which risk does the bank face <strong>because of the loan itself</strong>?</p>", "options": [["A", "Funding liquidity risk"], ["B", "Credit risk"], ["C", "Market directional risk"], ["D", "Resource risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Ask \"who owes whom money?\" The developer owes the bank, so the bank faces <strong>credit risk</strong>: the chance of non-payment or late payment. Funding liquidity risk is about the bank's own ability to pay its obligations.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor holds shares in 80 companies across unrelated industries. Bank Negara raises interest rates sharply and almost all holdings fall. Which risk materialised?</p>", "options": [["A", "Diversifiable risk"], ["B", "Particular risk"], ["C", "Pure risk"], ["D", "Non-diversifiable (systematic) risk"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>An economy-wide rate shock affects all firms, so diversification cannot remove it: <strong>systematic</strong> risk. Diversifiable and particular risk are firm-specific; pure risk is about outcome structure, and share investment is speculative.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>An airline hedges 70% of next year's jet-fuel needs. In Topic 1 terms, which risk is it mainly managing?</p>", "options": [["A", "Pure risk via loss prevention"], ["B", "Reputational risk"], ["C", "Credit risk"], ["D", "Market (price) risk, reducing the severity of an adverse price move"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Fuel-price movements are market risk; hedging does not stop prices rising but limits the financial impact, a severity-reducing response. The slides note hedging one speculative exposure does not make it a pure risk.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Auditor", "question": "<p>An internal auditor finds that one clerk can create vendors and approve payments to them. Which risk and control idea is most relevant?</p>", "options": [["A", "Market risk; diversification"], ["B", "Systematic risk; beta"], ["C", "Credit risk; collateral"], ["D", "Operational (fraud) risk; segregate duties as loss prevention"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Combining incompatible duties creates fraud opportunity, an operational risk on the slides. Segregation of duties makes fraud less likely, which is prevention.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A flawed credit-scoring model approves many borrowers who later default. What is the <strong>root</strong> risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "Credit risk, because borrowers defaulted"], ["B", "Operational (model) risk, with credit losses as the consequence"], ["C", "Market risk, because loan values fell"], ["D", "Reputational risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides place <strong>model risk</strong> under operational risk. The defaults look like credit losses, but the cause is a flawed internal model. Root cause and consequence are different; A names only the consequence.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A company owns RM100m of property, holds RM50,000 cash and must pay RM2m tomorrow. The property would take months to sell. Which pair of risks best describes the position?</p>", "options": [["A", "Credit risk and market risk"], ["B", "Business risk and resource risk"], ["C", "Funding liquidity risk and asset liquidity risk"], ["D", "Systematic risk and pure risk"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>It may not meet obligations when due (<strong>funding</strong> liquidity) and cannot sell the asset quickly without loss (<strong>asset</strong> liquidity). Wealth ≠ liquidity. Nobody owes it money (not credit), and the issue is not demand (business).</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Risk A is a 1% chance of a RM100m loss; Risk B is a certain RM1m loss. Which conclusion is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "A may be more dangerous for a small firm because its extreme outcome could threaten survival, despite equal expected loss"], ["B", "B is worse because it is certain"], ["C", "They are equivalent because expected losses are equal"], ["D", "Neither matters because expected loss is only RM1m"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Both have expected loss of RM1m, but A's tail outcome could bankrupt a small company. Serious analysis looks at the <strong>distribution and extreme outcomes</strong>, not just averages. C ignores severity; B and D misread the comparison.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A ride-hailing app's servers fail on New Year's Eve. Drivers cannot earn, riders switch to rivals, and the hashtag trends. Which is the best primary classification?</p>", "options": [["A", "Reputational risk, because it trended"], ["B", "Operational (technology failure) risk as the root, with business and reputational consequences"], ["C", "Market risk"], ["D", "Systematic risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The source is a technology failure in operations; lost trips and the trending hashtag are consequences. Analysis-level answers name the root, then the cascade.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>A fund holds 50 Malaysian palm-oil producers and claims to be \"well diversified\". The flaw?</p>", "options": [["A", "50 is too few assets for any diversification"], ["B", "The holdings share the same drivers (commodity prices, weather, regulation), so firm-specific risk does not cancel well"], ["C", "Diversification only works with bonds"], ["D", "Palm oil is a pure risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Diversification needs assets that do not all react identically. Quantity alone is not enough when risk drivers are common.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>Why does the slides' \"systematic risk\" page describing interlinked institutions and cascading failure cause confusion?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because systematic and systemic risk are identical"], ["B", "Because systemic risk is diversifiable"], ["C", "Because it describes systemic (contagion) risk while labelled systematic (market-wide, non-diversifiable) risk; related but distinct concepts"], ["D", "Because cascading failure is a pure risk"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Systematic risk is broad market exposure; systemic risk is failure propagating through a connected system. Follow the lecturer's wording in exams but keep the concepts apart.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D13", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A firm considers avoiding all overseas expansion to eliminate political and FX risk. What is the strongest evaluation?</p>", "options": [["A", "Avoidance reduces the chance of those losses to zero but also forgoes the upside; management should compare expected value, risk capacity and the cost of prevention and reduction alternatives"], ["B", "Correct: avoidance is always the safest and therefore best choice"], ["C", "Avoidance is impossible for political risk"], ["D", "Avoidance is equivalent to loss prevention"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Avoidance removes the exposure and its upside together. A good decision weighs reward against risk and controls. B treats safety as the only objective; C is false; D confuses the techniques.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D14", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>After a boycott, KFC Malaysia temporarily closes outlets. Which answer earns the most marks?</p>", "options": [["A", "\"Operational risk, because outlets closed.\""], ["B", "\"Primarily business risk (falling demand from changing customer sentiment), with a reputational component; closures are an operational consequence rather than the source.\""], ["C", "\"Reputational risk.\""], ["D", "\"Credit risk, because revenue fell.\""]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The strong answer separates <strong>cause → risk → consequence</strong>: the demand driver is business risk, brand perception adds reputational risk, and closures are the consequence. A and C pick one word; D misuses credit risk.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D15", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A single-supplier EV-battery maker must choose one control first. Which choice is best justified?</p>", "options": [["A", "Avoidance: stop using the mineral entirely"], ["B", "Accept the risk because suppliers rarely fail"], ["C", "Prevention: qualify additional suppliers so one failure is less likely to stop production, backed by safety stock as reduction"], ["D", "Rely only on insurance after a disruption"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Supplier diversification lowers the <strong>frequency</strong> of production stoppages, and safety stock reduces <strong>severity</strong> if disruption occurs. Avoidance is unrealistic for a core input; B ignores a severe dependency; D addresses only part of the post-loss consequence.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Auditor", "question": "<p>A firm reports \"zero fire losses in ten years\" and argues fire is no longer a risk. The auditor's best response?</p>", "options": [["A", "Agree: historical data proves the risk is gone"], ["B", "Disagree: the loss exposure still exists; low historical frequency does not remove a severe exposure"], ["C", "Agree, but only for pure risks"], ["D", "Disagree, because fire is speculative"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A loss exposure exists regardless of whether a loss has occurred. Rare, severe events are exactly what historical averages understate.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Board", "question": "<p>A board debates a project with expected return 15% and moderate risk versus 4% and low risk. Which board question is most consistent with Topic 1?</p>", "options": [["A", "\"Which project has lower risk?\""], ["B", "\"Which project avoids all uncertainty?\""], ["C", "\"How does each affect our objectives, and can we bear the downside of the 15% project?\""], ["D", "\"Which project is easiest to insure?\""]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Risk only makes sense relative to objectives; the decision weighs upside against downside and capacity. A and B treat risk as purely bad; D narrows the question to one tool.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>A researcher proposes ranking risks by expected loss (frequency x severity) only. The strongest critique?</p>", "options": [["A", "Expected loss is too hard to compute"], ["B", "Severity cannot be estimated"], ["C", "Frequency is irrelevant to risk"], ["D", "It ignores the distribution: low-frequency, high-severity events with equal expected loss may threaten survival, so tail measures and capacity matter"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Equal averages can hide catastrophic tails (Risk A vs Risk B in the notes). Survival depends on extreme outcomes relative to capacity, not just the mean.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Under the broader definition, should an ERM framework track upside deviations (opportunities) as rigorously as downside ones?</p>", "options": [["A", "No: risk management is only about losses"], ["B", "Only for pure risks"], ["C", "Yes, to the extent they affect objectives: unmanaged upside can create capacity, quality and supply problems, and missed opportunities are value lost"], ["D", "Only if regulators require it"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Risk is the effect of uncertainty on objectives, positive or negative. Ignoring upside leaves the organisation unprepared and blind to opportunity.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D16", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which sequence best designs a risk process for a firm entering a new market, consistent with Topic 1?</p>", "options": [["A", "Choose treatments → identify exposures → set objectives → monitor"], ["B", "Classify risks → choose avoidance for all → report"], ["C", "Buy insurance → assess frequency → set objectives → classify risks"], ["D", "Set objectives → identify exposures → classify risks → assess frequency/severity → prioritise → choose treatments → monitor"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Objectives must come first (risk is relative to them), then identification, classification, sizing, prioritisation, treatment and monitoring. The other orders put treatment before understanding, or avoid everything.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D17", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You must classify a single factory fire for a board report. Which framing best reflects the chapter's \"classifications overlap\" insight?</p>", "options": [["A", "Choose exactly one category so the report is unambiguous"], ["B", "Report it only as speculative risk"], ["C", "Present it by dimension: pure (outcome), diversifiable (portfolio view), operational (where it hits), plus liquidity and reputational consequences"], ["D", "Report it as systematic risk because it affects the economy"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Classifications answer different questions, so one event can carry several labels. Naming the dimension is analysis-level work. A loses information; B and D are wrong classifications.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D18", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which identification plan would most completely find exposures in a manufacturing firm?</p>", "options": [["A", "Combine checklists, physical inspection, flowcharts, financial statements and historical loss data"], ["B", "Review historical losses only"], ["C", "Use the balance sheet only, because all exposures appear there"], ["D", "Ask the insurer to list risks"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Each method looks from a different direction: inspections find blocked exits, flowcharts single suppliers, statements debt exposure, history control weaknesses. Any single source misses something.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Board", "question": "<p>Which dashboard design best follows the Topic 1 risk chain for a board?</p>", "options": [["A", "For each key objective: exposures, events, frequency/severity estimates, consequences, chosen control (avoid/prevent/reduce) and owner"], ["B", "A list of risk names coloured red, amber or green"], ["C", "Only last year's actual losses"], ["D", "Only the insurance schedule"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Objective → exposure → event → frequency/severity → consequence → response is the chain the chapter builds. B, C and D capture fragments.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Design principle: a firm wants one taxonomy that avoids double counting yet captures cascades. Which approach fits Topic 1 best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Classify each event by its root source, record its outcome structure and diversifiability as attributes, and link downstream consequences as separate, connected entries"], ["B", "Assign every event to exactly one category and ignore consequences"], ["C", "Use only pure vs speculative"], ["D", "Use only enterprise categories"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Separating root source from consequences, while keeping the other dimensions as attributes, preserves the \"classifications answer different questions\" insight and supports cascade analysis without double counting.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which list contains only the five Malaysian business forms named on the slides?</p>", "options": [["A", "Sole proprietorship, partnership, cooperative, Sdn. Bhd., Berhad"], ["B", "Sole proprietorship, partnership, LLP, Sdn. Bhd., Berhad"], ["C", "Partnership, LLC, Sdn. Bhd., Berhad, trust"], ["D", "Sole proprietorship, LLP, Sdn. Bhd., Berhad, government-linked company"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides list <strong>sole proprietorship, partnership, limited liability partnership (LLP), private limited company (Sdn. Bhd.) and public limited company (Berhad)</strong>. Cooperatives, trusts, LLCs (a US form) and GLCs are not in that list.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which is a <strong>post-loss</strong> objective of risk management?</p>", "options": [["A", "Reduce anxiety"], ["B", "Meet legal obligations"], ["C", "Prepare for potential losses in the most economical way"], ["D", "Stabilise earnings"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Post-loss objectives: survival, continuity of operations, <strong>stable earnings</strong>, continued growth, and minimising effects on people and society. Reducing anxiety, meeting legal obligations and economical preparation are pre-loss objectives.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>On the slides' enterprise-risk pyramid, at which level do credit, market, IT, marketing and competitive risk appear?</p>", "options": [["A", "Strategic"], ["B", "Operational"], ["C", "Tactical"], ["D", "Compliance"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slide lists these under <strong>tactical</strong> risk: risks that affect how the strategy is executed. Strategic risk concerns direction; operational risk daily execution; compliance risk legal permission.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>What does \"limited liability\" change for a Sdn. Bhd. shareholder?</p>", "options": [["A", "It reduces the company's operational and market risks"], ["B", "It caps the shareholder's exposure at roughly the amount invested, because the company is a separate legal entity"], ["C", "It means the company cannot be sued"], ["D", "It makes the shareholder responsible for management decisions"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Limited liability moves the boundary of <strong>who bears the loss</strong>; the company's own business risks are unchanged and it can still be sued. Shareholders do not manage the company by virtue of owning shares.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why did insurance-based risk management prove insufficient?</p>", "options": [["A", "Insurance is too expensive for all firms"], ["B", "Insurers refused to cover fire and flood"], ["C", "Regulators banned insurance-based approaches"], ["D", "Many important risks, such as poor strategy, reputation and changing customer behaviour, cannot simply be insured"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Insurance handles pure risks like fire and theft well, but uninsurable risks were neglected. You cannot claim from an insurer because management misread the market.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>What is the main weakness of the silo approach?</p>", "options": [["A", "Each department manages its own risk, so correlated risks and dependencies across the enterprise can be missed"], ["B", "Specialists are less skilled than generalists"], ["C", "It manages too many risks at once"], ["D", "It ignores financial risks"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Silo reports can each be accurate yet miss that several risks depend on the same driver. Total enterprise risk ≠ the sum of isolated conclusions. Silos actually concentrated on financial and physical risks.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>In portfolio terms, why can several \"acceptable\" risks produce an unacceptable total?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because variances never add"], ["B", "Because expected values change when you add risks"], ["C", "Because insurance premiums rise"], ["D", "Because positive correlation raises the probability of joint losses, so the combined distribution has a fatter tail than independent risks would"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>With correlated risks, P(several losses together) rises sharply; independence assumptions understate tail exposure. This is the quantitative idea behind ERM aggregation.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>After a flood, a manufacturer restarts production at a backup site within a week and keeps paying staff. Which objectives is it pursuing?</p>", "options": [["A", "Pre-loss: reduce anxiety and meet legal obligations"], ["B", "Pre-loss: economical preparation"], ["C", "Post-loss: survival and continuity of operations"], ["D", "Neither; this is loss prevention"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The loss has happened, so these are <strong>post-loss</strong> objectives: survive and continue operating (and stabilise earnings). Prevention would have acted before the flood.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Three accountants want to share management while protecting each partner from the others' professional mistakes. Which structure fits best?</p>", "options": [["A", "LLP"], ["B", "General partnership"], ["C", "Sole proprietorship"], ["D", "Berhad"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>An LLP combines partnership flexibility with company-like protection: partners are generally liable only to the extent of their investment. A general partnership exposes partners to joint and several liability; a Berhad is a listed company.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A bank decides to exit retail lending and focus on wealth management. Its accounting system later fails during the transition. Classify the two risks.</p>", "options": [["A", "Both strategic"], ["B", "Both operational"], ["C", "Strategic and tactical"], ["D", "Strategic (the change of direction) and operational (the system failure)"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Changing the business model is a <strong>strategic</strong> decision; a system failure in daily execution is <strong>operational</strong>. Keep decision direction separate from execution failure.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A Malaysian SME bakery is run by one owner who personally guarantees a bank loan. If the bakery fails, what is the owner's exposure?</p>", "options": [["A", "Personal assets may be used to repay the business debt"], ["B", "Only the bakery's equipment"], ["C", "Nothing, because banks bear business risk"], ["D", "Only the share capital"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A sole proprietor has unlimited liability, and a personal guarantee makes the exposure explicit: business risk becomes personal risk.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Four departments each assess their risk as \"low\", but all depend on stable China-US trade. Why can the enterprise still be in danger?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because departmental analysts are usually wrong"], ["B", "Because a common driver makes the risks correlated, so a single shock can trigger several losses at once"], ["C", "Because low risks are always underestimated"], ["D", "Because ERM requires every risk to be rated high"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The failure is not bad departmental analysis but <strong>dependency</strong>: correlated risks materialise together. ERM aggregates risks to see that combined exposure.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A manager cuts maintenance and quality checks to hit this year's profit target. What does Topic 2 say about this?</p>", "options": [["A", "It maximises shareholder wealth because profit rises"], ["B", "It has no risk implications"], ["C", "Short-term profit may rise while operational, quality and reputation risk increase, so it can reduce long-term shareholder wealth"], ["D", "It is a compliance issue only"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Objectives can conflict: cost minimisation can raise operational risk. Shareholder wealth is long-term value, not this year's profit, which is why risk management cannot be separated from strategy.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why does separating ownership from control in a corporation create new risks even as it solves others?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because shareholders become personally liable"], ["B", "Because corporations cannot raise capital"], ["C", "Because management always acts against shareholders"], ["D", "Because managers may pursue goals (e.g. short-term bonuses) that differ from owners' long-term interests, creating agency and governance risk"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Limited liability and professional management solve concentration problems, but owners and managers can have different objectives, plus information asymmetry. \"Always\" overstates it; shareholders are not personally liable.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>An airline launches a new regional route network. Which item is a <strong>tactical</strong> rather than strategic risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "Whether the new region will be profitable at all"], ["B", "Whether to change the business model to long-haul"], ["C", "Fuel hedging and aircraft financing for the launch"], ["D", "Whether to exit the region entirely"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Financing and hedging are about <strong>executing</strong> the chosen strategy. Profitability of the region, business-model changes and exit decisions are strategic direction.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Auditor", "question": "<p>An internal auditor finds that credit, liquidity and market risk are each reported by a different team using different scales, with no combined view. What is the main finding?</p>", "options": [["A", "The firm is fully compliant, so no issue exists"], ["B", "A silo approach: the board cannot see aggregated, correlated exposure"], ["C", "Too much risk reporting"], ["D", "The teams should be merged into IT"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Different scales and no aggregation is the classic conventional-CRM silo problem the slides describe.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Why might holistic RM integrate sustainability rather than leave it to a CSR team?</p>", "options": [["A", "Climate change, for example, drives physical, supply-chain, market, regulatory, financial, reputational and strategic risks at once"], ["B", "Sustainability is legally required to be in the risk department"], ["C", "CSR teams are too expensive"], ["D", "Sustainability risks are always small"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>An environmental driver becomes multiple enterprise risks. Managing it separately recreates the silo problem.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>Which statement about technology in modern risk management is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Technology only reduces risk"], ["B", "Technology only creates risk"], ["C", "Technology is irrelevant to ERM"], ["D", "Analytics and AI improve identification, modelling and real-time monitoring, but also create model, cyber, data-quality and dependency risks"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A control can itself become a source of risk. The slides note technology's role in risk modelling, scenario analysis and monitoring; the double effect is the higher-order insight.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D13", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A rapidly expanding manufacturer asks whether to keep traditional RM or adopt ERM. Which answer is strongest?</p>", "options": [["A", "Always adopt ERM because it is holistic"], ["B", "Keep traditional RM because it is cheaper"], ["C", "Traditional RM may suit narrow, well-defined exposures, but growth creates dependencies and strategic complexity that make ERM more suitable"], ["D", "Adopt neither; buy more insurance"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Evaluate-level answers give conditions and a mechanism, not slogans. As interconnections grow, silo management misses aggregated exposure, which is what ERM addresses.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D14", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Project A offers 25% expected return with significant risk; Project B offers 5% with low risk. What should risk management conclude?</p>", "options": [["A", "Choose B because it is safer"], ["B", "Evaluate whether A's risk is understood, within capacity and appetite, controllable and compensated by its return before deciding"], ["C", "Choose A because higher return is always better"], ["D", "Reject both because both carry risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Risk management improves decisions under uncertainty; it does not automatically pick the safest option. Choosing B by reflex is avoidance disguised as expertise.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D15", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A debt-funded acquisition carries IT, single-supplier and environmental risks. The board asks: abandon it? Best response?</p>", "options": [["A", "Yes, because there are many risks"], ["B", "No, because expected market share is high"], ["C", "Only if insurance is unavailable"], ["D", "Not necessarily: treat the major risks, then compare the aggregated residual risk with the expected strategic return"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>\"Many risks\" is not a decision rule. ERM maps how the risks interact (supplier failure → production stop → revenue fall → debt pressure) and decides on residual risk versus value.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Auditor", "question": "<p>A company's risk register was completed in January and has not been updated since. The auditor should conclude:</p>", "options": [["A", "The process is complete"], ["B", "Only insurance coverage matters"], ["C", "The register is too detailed"], ["D", "Risk management is a cycle; without monitoring and review the register no longer reflects current risks"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Monitoring feeds back into identification. A static register is documentation, not risk management.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Board", "question": "<p>A board proposes a policy to \"eliminate all risk from operations\". The best board-level critique?</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct: ERM aims to eliminate risk"], ["B", "Eliminating all risk would also eliminate investment, innovation and growth; the aim is appropriate risk for appropriate expected value"], ["C", "It should eliminate only reputational risk"], ["D", "It should transfer all risk to insurers instead"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>No risk ≠ optimal management. ERM optimises risk against value; total transfer is also neither possible nor desirable.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>Does the chapter's stage model imply older risks disappear as RM evolves?</p>", "options": [["A", "Yes: insurance risk disappeared once ERM arrived"], ["B", "No: risk categories accumulated, so modern firms still face fire risk plus cyber, AI/model, ESG, geopolitical and supply-chain risk"], ["C", "Yes: only strategic risk remains"], ["D", "No: but only financial risks remain"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The sequence insurance → market/financial → operational → strategic shows an expanding risk universe, not replacement.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Critically, what is the strongest justification for ERM over traditional RM?</p>", "options": [["A", "ERM manages more risk types"], ["B", "ERM uses more spreadsheets"], ["C", "The effect of one risk cannot always be understood independently of other risks or of corporate strategy"], ["D", "ERM removes the need for governance"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>\"More risk types\" is true but shallow. The deep justification is interaction and strategy-dependence, which require aggregation against objectives.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D16", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which design best reflects ERM for a multinational entering a new market?</p>", "options": [["A", "Each department writes its own risk register; the board sees totals once a year"], ["B", "Objectives → identification → strategic/tactical/operational/compliance assessment → risk owners → cross-risk aggregation → treatment → monitoring and reporting → board review"], ["C", "Buy insurance for all assets, then proceed"], ["D", "Let the risk department own every risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>ERM links objectives to all levels of risk, assigns owners, aggregates across risks and reports to the board. The other options are silo, insurance-only or ownership-confusion designs.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D17", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which statement best captures the chapter's evolutionary logic in one sentence?</p>", "options": [["A", "Risk management moved from reactive insurance of individual losses, through departmental and financial RM, to integrated enterprise-wide management of interconnected risks for long-term value"], ["B", "Risk management moved from ERM back to insurance as markets matured"], ["C", "Risk management has always been holistic"], ["D", "Risk management evolved to eliminate all risk"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Each stage arose because the previous one became insufficient as organisations grew larger and more interconnected; ERM aims at long-term value, not zero risk.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D18", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>How should the roles of risk owners and the risk function be designed?</p>", "options": [["A", "The risk function owns and manages every risk"], ["B", "Business units ignore risk because the risk function handles it"], ["C", "Business units own and manage the risks they generate; the risk function provides framework, measurement, challenge and reporting"], ["D", "The board manages all operational risks directly"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Operations understands machinery, treasury understands cash. Otherwise everyone takes risks and hands the consequences to the risk department.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Board", "question": "<p>Which board agenda item best shows <strong>integrated</strong> (not traditional) risk management for a proposed acquisition?</p>", "options": [["A", "Approve the deal, then ask the risk team to insure the target's assets"], ["B", "Ask finance alone to hedge the currency"], ["C", "Review strategic, FX, regulatory, supply-chain and reputational risks together before deciding whether to proceed"], ["D", "Delegate the decision entirely to the CFO"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Integrated RM brings risk into the strategic decision itself, across categories, rather than cleaning up afterwards.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Design a governance-light way for a mid-sized firm to capture correlation without a full ERM department. Which is most consistent with Topic 2?</p>", "options": [["A", "Keep separate registers but add no common scale"], ["B", "Outsource all risks to an insurer"], ["C", "Rate every risk \"high\" to be safe"], ["D", "A common risk scale and register, a quarterly cross-functional review that maps shared drivers (e.g. one market, one supplier), and aggregated reporting to the board"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Shared scales, explicit dependency mapping and aggregation address the silo weakness proportionately; the other options either keep silos, transfer blindly or destroy information.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which list gives the five elements of corporate governance on the slides?</p>", "options": [["A", "Accountability, transparency, regulatory framework, business ethics and social responsibility, administrative structure"], ["B", "Accountability, profitability, compliance, auditing, marketing"], ["C", "Transparency, risk transfer, line management, analytics, technology"], ["D", "Objectives, components, levels, monitoring, reporting"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The five elements each solve a governance problem: who is responsible, information asymmetry, rules of the game, legal-but-wrong conduct, and who reports to whom. The other lists mix in building blocks or COSO terms.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>The Sarbanes-Oxley Act was enacted in response to which events?</p>", "options": [["A", "The 1MDB scandal"], ["B", "Corporate frauds such as Enron and WorldCom"], ["C", "The 2008 global financial crisis"], ["D", "The Volkswagen emissions scandal"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>SOX (2002) followed high-profile frauds including Enron and WorldCom, aiming to restore trust in corporate disclosures.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which body is associated with the Green Book for government-linked companies?</p>", "options": [["A", "Bank Negara Malaysia"], ["B", "Bursa Malaysia"], ["C", "Putrajaya Committee on GLC High Performance"], ["D", "Securities Commission"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slide maps the Green Book (enhancing board effectiveness) to the PCG for GLCs. BNM covers financial institutions; SC and Bursa cover public listed companies.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>What is the best distinction between management and governance?</p>", "options": [["A", "Management is done by shareholders; governance by staff"], ["B", "Governance is about daily operations; management about oversight"], ["C", "They are the same function at different sizes of company"], ["D", "Management runs daily operations; governance is the system by which the firm is directed, controlled and held accountable"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Management = running the business; governance = making sure it is run properly. Swapping the two is the most common trap.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>What does it mean for risk governance to be \"embedded\"?</p>", "options": [["A", "Risk considerations actually shape culture, processes, incentives and decisions, not just policy documents"], ["B", "The risk policy is stored in the company intranet"], ["C", "Every employee is a member of the risk committee"], ["D", "The board writes all risk procedures"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides define risk governance as embedding RM and control frameworks into culture, processes and structure. A policy contradicted by bonuses is not embedded.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why is ISO 31000 described as \"not for certification\"?</p>", "options": [["A", "It has been withdrawn"], ["B", "It only applies to banks"], ["C", "It provides principles and guidelines to adapt, rather than auditable requirements to certify against"], ["D", "It is a law, so certification is unnecessary"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>ISO 31000 is guidance: principles, a framework and a process, tailored to each organisation. It is not a law, and it is generic across sectors.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Why does SOX focus so heavily on the reliability of financial information?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because investors allocate capital based on disclosures; false information causes bad capital allocation and loss of market trust"], ["B", "Because SOX is an ERM framework"], ["C", "Because operational risk does not exist"], ["D", "Because it replaced accounting standards"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>SOX attacks information asymmetry: bad information → bad capital allocation. It is not an ERM framework.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Every payment above RM1 million requires two authorised signatures. In COSO terms this is:</p>", "options": [["A", "Internal environment"], ["B", "Control activities"], ["C", "Event identification"], ["D", "Objective setting"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A dual-authorisation rule makes a risk response actually operate: a <strong>control activity</strong>.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A commercial bank wants the governance and risk-governance guidelines that apply to it. Which regulator?</p>", "options": [["A", "Bank Negara Malaysia"], ["B", "Bursa Malaysia"], ["C", "Securities Commission, via the Green Book"], ["D", "SSM"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>BNM issues guidelines for financial institutions. The Green Book is the PCG's, for GLCs; Bursa guides listed issuers; SSM administers company law.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Legal, risk and internal-audit teams each keep separate registers for the same new-market entry, duplicating work and missing overlaps. Which concept addresses this?</p>", "options": [["A", "Systemic risk"], ["B", "Risk transfer"], ["C", "Business ethics"], ["D", "GRC (governance, risk and compliance integration)"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>GRC combines governance, risk management and compliance to remove silo mentality and blind spots.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A Malaysian listed company's annual report must describe its principal risks and the systems to manage them. Which code underpins this expectation?</p>", "options": [["A", "SOX"], ["B", "ISO Guide 73"], ["C", "MCCG"], ["D", "AS/NZS 4360"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The MCCG principle linked to ERM is \"identify principal risks and implement appropriate systems to manage them\"; Bursa and the SC apply it to listed companies.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Auditor", "question": "<p>Which ISO companion document would an auditor cite for agreed risk-management terminology?</p>", "options": [["A", "ISO/IEC 31010"], ["B", "ISO 9001"], ["C", "AS/NZS 4360"], ["D", "ISO Guide 73"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Guide 73 provides risk-management vocabulary; ISO/IEC 31010 covers assessment techniques.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A company has a complete risk framework, but directors routinely ignore risk reports when approving deals. What is the core problem?</p>", "options": [["A", "The framework is too detailed"], ["B", "Weak risk governance: the framework is not embedded in decision making and oversight"], ["C", "A lack of insurance"], ["D", "Excessive transparency"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Formal documentation exists, but risk information does not influence decisions. That is a governance failure, not a framework-content failure.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>The CEO and CFO collude to approve a fraudulent RM5m payment despite a dual-signature rule. What does this show?</p>", "options": [["A", "Dual signatures are useless"], ["B", "The firm should remove all controls"], ["C", "Controls reduce risk but do not guarantee zero risk; independent oversight, audit and culture are needed above them"], ["D", "Fraud is a market risk"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Controls operate within a governance system. Collusion by the people a control assumes are independent defeats it, so governance mechanisms must sit above controls.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A board approves a RM3bn acquisition after a short presentation; several directors have ties to the target's owners and due diligence was rushed. Which pair of governance elements is most clearly violated?</p>", "options": [["A", "Accountability and transparency (with ethics, given the conflicts)"], ["B", "Regulatory framework only"], ["C", "Administrative structure only"], ["D", "None, if the deal is legal"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The board lacked full information (transparency), nobody challenged the assumptions (accountability), and conflicts of interest raise ethics. Legality does not establish good governance.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A plant manager knows a reactor is becoming unstable, but the board's dashboard shows green. Which COSO component failed?</p>", "options": [["A", "Information and communication"], ["B", "Objective setting"], ["C", "Risk response"], ["D", "Internal environment only"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Risk information must travel up, down and across. A green dashboard over a known hazard is an information failure, not a measurement success.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Which of the seven building blocks most directly addresses correlation between FX, supplier and credit risks?</p>", "options": [["A", "Risk transfer"], ["B", "Stakeholder management"], ["C", "Data and technology"], ["D", "Portfolio management"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Portfolio management treats risks collectively, the \"fund manager\" view of combined exposure, linking Topic 3 to Topic 2's ERM logic.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>Why might Malaysia rely on a governance code plus listing requirements rather than a SOX-style statute?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because Malaysia has no regulators"], ["B", "A principles-based code with listing enforcement allows proportionate, adaptable application while still setting expectations"], ["C", "Because codes are stricter than law"], ["D", "Because SOX applies in Malaysia"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides note there is no SOX-like ERM law; the MCCG and Bursa requirements guide behaviour. Codes trade legal force for flexibility, which is why their content keeps evolving.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D13", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>\"Our company complies with every reporting rule, so our ERM is strong.\" Evaluate.</p>", "options": [["A", "Agree: compliance is sufficient for ERM"], ["B", "Agree, provided SOX applies"], ["C", "Disagree: compliance never matters"], ["D", "Disagree: compliance is necessary but not sufficient; a compliant firm can still make value-destroying strategic decisions or neglect emerging risks"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A legally approved, properly disclosed acquisition can still overpay massively. Good ERM manages strategic, operational and emerging risks beyond the legal minimum.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D14", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>In the rushed acquisition case, what should the board do?</p>", "options": [["A", "Approve, because the CEO strongly supports it"], ["B", "Reject it permanently because it is risky"], ["C", "Not approve it in its current information state; require independent due diligence, conflict management, a revised valuation, debt stress testing, a cyber assessment and a formal risk report, then reconsider"], ["D", "Delegate the decision to the risk team"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Everything interesting is risky; the issue is an uninformed, conflicted decision. Conditional judgement with specific remedies is the evaluate-level answer.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D15", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which comparison of COSO and ISO 31000 is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "COSO frames ERM as objectives x components x organisational levels; ISO 31000 as principles + framework + process; they overlap but package the ideas differently"], ["B", "COSO is a law; ISO is a framework"], ["C", "ISO 31000 is only for certification; COSO is not"], ["D", "They are identical documents from different countries"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Neither is a law (SOX is). ISO is not for certification. They share goals but differ in structure: COSO = enterprise architecture, ISO = principles, integration and process.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Auditor", "question": "<p>An internal audit team both designs payment controls and audits them. Best evaluation?</p>", "options": [["A", "Efficient and acceptable"], ["B", "Independence is compromised: assurance should be separate from the design and operation of controls"], ["C", "Required by SOX"], ["D", "Only a problem for small companies"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Assurance must be independent to be credible; marking your own homework is the governance problem the chapter opens with.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Board", "question": "<p>A board spends meetings deciding maintenance schedules and staff rosters. What is wrong?</p>", "options": [["A", "Nothing; detail shows diligence"], ["B", "It should also write the code"], ["C", "It is micromanaging execution instead of overseeing major risks, controls, incentives and escalation"], ["D", "Boards should never discuss operations"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Board = oversight; management = execution. Confusing them produces meddling boards while strategic oversight is neglected.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>What does the MCCG's revision history (2000, 2007, 2012, 2017, 2021) most strongly suggest?</p>", "options": [["A", "The code failed and was repeatedly replaced"], ["B", "Revisions were cosmetic"], ["C", "Governance frameworks are dynamic; they evolve with the risk environment, e.g. stronger board oversight and sustainability integration"], ["D", "Only dates matter in exams"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>New risks such as cyber and sustainability push regulation to evolve; the direction is stronger boards and broader scope.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Is \"following COSO\" sufficient evidence of good risk management?</p>", "options": [["A", "Yes: COSO guarantees effectiveness"], ["B", "No: frameworks structure good practice, but mechanical application without culture, information flow and commitment gives \"beautiful templates\" and poor outcomes"], ["C", "Yes, if combined with SOX"], ["D", "No, because COSO is outdated"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Following a framework mechanically does not guarantee good risk management; effectiveness depends on embedding, which is a governance question.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D16", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which governance design for a listed company best reflects Topic 3?</p>", "options": [["A", "CEO approves all risks; the board receives an annual summary"], ["B", "Shareholders → board → audit/risk committees → management → risk owners, with policies, controls, reporting, assurance and monitoring"], ["C", "The risk department reports only to the CFO"], ["D", "Internal audit designs and runs all controls"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>It separates oversight from execution, creates independent committees, assigns owners and includes assurance. Auditors running controls would destroy their independence.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D17", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which sequence applies AS/NZS 4360 correctly to a new product launch?</p>", "options": [["A", "Treat risks → identify risks → establish context → monitor"], ["B", "Identify risks → treat → establish context"], ["C", "Establish context → identify → assess and prioritise → treat → monitor and review, with communication and consultation throughout"], ["D", "Monitor → treat → report"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Context comes first: whether 10% volatility is high depends on objectives, environment and stakeholders.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D18", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which ISO 31000 framework sequence would you implement, and why does it start where it does?</p>", "options": [["A", "Design → implement → mandate and commitment → review"], ["B", "Implement → design → improve"], ["C", "Monitor → design → commit"], ["D", "Mandate and commitment → design → implement → monitor and review → continual improvement; without leadership commitment the framework is ornamental"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Budget, staff, board attention and reporting authority must come first; otherwise the remaining steps have no force.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Board", "question": "<p>Which incentive change best embeds risk governance at a plant where bonuses reward volume only?</p>", "options": [["A", "Add a safety poster"], ["B", "Balance bonuses with safety and control metrics, and make line managers accountable for risk outcomes"], ["C", "Remove all bonuses"], ["D", "Ask the risk team to inspect more often"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Incentives reveal the real culture. Linking pay and accountability to risk outcomes embeds governance; posters do not.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Design an integrated architecture for a listed Malaysian group. Which combination best uses the chapter's tools?</p>", "options": [["A", "SOX alone"], ["B", "Insurance plus annual audit only"], ["C", "MCCG-driven board oversight, COSO to structure ERM across objectives, components and levels, ISO 31000 principles to embed RM in decisions, GRC to integrate compliance, and independent assurance"], ["D", "ISO 31000 certification only"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Each tool solves a different problem: the code sets governance expectations, COSO provides architecture, ISO embeds RM in decisions, GRC removes silos and assurance verifies. ISO 31000 is not certifiable, and SOX is US law.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>How many goals and targets make up the 2015 UN Sustainable Development Goals?</p>", "options": [["A", "8 goals, 21 targets"], ["B", "17 goals, 169 targets"], ["C", "15 goals, 100 targets"], ["D", "5 goals, 17 targets"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The 2030 Agenda has <strong>17 goals and 169 targets</strong>, organised around the 5Ps. The 2000 MDGs had 8 goals.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>The Triple Bottom Line consists of:</p>", "options": [["A", "People, planet, prosperity"], ["B", "People, planet, partnership"], ["C", "People, planet, profit"], ["D", "Profit, prosperity, peace"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>John Elkington's TBL = people, planet, profit. Prosperity, peace and partnership belong to the SDG 5Ps.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>When was the FTSE4Good Bursa Malaysia Index launched, and from which universe are constituents drawn?</p>", "options": [["A", "December 2014; FTSE Bursa Malaysia EMAS"], ["B", "2021; FBM KLCI only"], ["C", "2004; all ASEAN exchanges"], ["D", "2015; government-linked companies only"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slide: launched December 2014 as the local exchange's ESG measure, drawing constituents from the FTSE Bursa Malaysia EMAS index.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why is sustainability <strong>not</strong> the same as environmentalism?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because sustainability ignores the environment"], ["B", "Because environmentalism is a legal requirement"], ["C", "Because sustainability only concerns profit"], ["D", "Because sustainability balances economic, environmental and social needs over time; a green but unviable firm, or a profitable firm using forced labour, is not sustainable"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Sustainability is a three-way balance across generations, not just environmental protection.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which best distinguishes SDGs from ESG?</p>", "options": [["A", "SDGs are global development goals; ESG concerns an organisation's environmental, social and governance factors"], ["B", "They are alternative names for the same thing"], ["C", "ESG contains 17 goals"], ["D", "SDGs apply only to companies"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The SDGs answer \"what outcomes should the world pursue?\"; ESG answers \"how is this organisation performing on or exposed to E, S and G?\". ESG actions can contribute to SDGs.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>What makes an ESG issue an <strong>ESG risk</strong> in the slides' sense?</p>", "options": [["A", "Any ESG topic mentioned by the media"], ["B", "It must be environmental"], ["C", "It is an E, S or G event or condition that could have a material negative impact on value"], ["D", "It must already have caused a loss"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The key word is <strong>material</strong>: capable of meaningfully affecting value. It need not have occurred, and it can be E, S or G.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Why does \"net zero\" not mean \"zero emissions\"?</p>", "options": [["A", "Net zero counts only Scope 1 emissions"], ["B", "Net zero balances emissions produced with emissions removed, so gross emissions can remain above zero"], ["C", "Net zero applies only to governments"], ["D", "They mean the same thing"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Gross emissions - removals = net emissions. Malaysia's 2050 carbon-neutrality target (announced September 2021) is a net concept.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A company reports \"tonnes of CO₂e emitted per year\". This is best classified as:</p>", "options": [["A", "An SDG"], ["B", "An ESG metric"], ["C", "ESG risk management"], ["D", "Greenwashing"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>It measures environmental performance: an ESG metric. SDG 13 is the global goal it may relate to.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A bank provides a loan to build a solar farm; separately, a fund buys shares in renewable-energy firms. Classify each.</p>", "options": [["A", "Both green investing"], ["B", "Both green finance"], ["C", "Green investing; green finance"], ["D", "Green finance; green investing"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Funding an environmental activity is <strong>green finance</strong>; an investor allocating capital to environmentally oriented firms is <strong>green investing</strong>.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Products from Malaysian glove and palm-oil firms were withheld by US Customs over forced-labour allegations. Which ESG pillar is the root issue?</p>", "options": [["A", "Social"], ["B", "Environmental"], ["C", "Governance"], ["D", "None; it is purely a trade issue"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Forced labour is a labour-standards (S) issue; the trade restriction and lost revenue are its consequences.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>For a semiconductor fab, which ESG issue is most likely highly material?</p>", "options": [["A", "Water availability"], ["B", "Lobbying disclosures only"], ["C", "Office paper use"], ["D", "Staff canteen menus"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Fabs are water-intensive; shortages can halt production. Materiality is business-specific.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Auditor", "question": "<p>An auditor reviews a reported 100,000 tCO₂e figure. Which question is most important?</p>", "options": [["A", "Is the report well designed?"], ["B", "How many photos are included?"], ["C", "Was the figure collected, bounded (including subsidiaries) and reviewed under controls that prevent manipulation?"], ["D", "Did marketing approve it?"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>ESG reporting quality = measurement + controls + governance, not graphic design.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A company's marketing claims a product is \"carbon neutral\", but nobody verified the data. Why is this as much a governance problem as an environmental one?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because emissions are not environmental"], ["B", "Because governance concerns only board size"], ["C", "Because the failure lies in who approved the claim, whether evidence was checked and whether controls existed"], ["D", "Because carbon neutrality is illegal"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Unverified claims reveal weak approval, verification and accountability: G questions. Consequences then spread to reputation, legal and financial risk.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Company A faces high ESG exposure but manages it well; Company B faces moderate exposure with almost no controls. What can you conclude?</p>", "options": [["A", "B is safer because exposure is lower"], ["B", "A is riskier in every case"], ["C", "Exposure and management quality are different; B may carry more unmanaged risk despite lower exposure"], ["D", "Both are equally risky"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The ASEAN comparison slide separates exposure from the ability to manage it. Assess both.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Trace the most likely chain from drought to a financial problem for a manufacturer.</p>", "options": [["A", "Drought → water shortage → production declines → revenue declines → cash flow deteriorates → liquidity pressure"], ["B", "Drought → higher share price → more investment"], ["C", "Drought → lower taxes → profit rises"], ["D", "Drought → greenwashing → fines"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>An environmental condition affecting the company becomes operational, then business, then liquidity risk: environmental risk becomes conventional financial risk.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>PETRONAS was excluded from certain JP Morgan ESG indices in 2021 due to a low ESG score. Which transmission mechanism does this illustrate?</p>", "options": [["A", "S → trade restriction → revenue"], ["B", "ESG performance → index eligibility → capital-market implications"], ["C", "G → executive pay → shareholder vote"], ["D", "E → flood → factory damage"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Index exclusion affects which investors can hold the securities, linking ESG performance to access to capital.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Why can capital markets change corporate ESG behaviour?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because investors ignore ESG"], ["B", "Because ESG has no effect on cost of capital"], ["C", "Because firms with unmanaged ESG liabilities may face higher required returns or exclusion, while credible practices can widen access to sustainable financing"], ["D", "Because ratings replace regulation"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Financial incentives → corporate ESG behaviour; this is why ESG moved onto CFO and board agendas and why sustainable bonds and sukuk grew.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>\"ESG problems rarely remain ESG problems.\" Which example best illustrates the G pathway?</p>", "options": [["A", "Drought → water shortage → output falls"], ["B", "Forced labour → import ban → lost revenue"], ["C", "Falsified financial statements → regulatory charges → share sell-down (Serba Dinamik)"], ["D", "Flood → factory damage"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>G → reporting credibility → regulatory, reputational and market consequences. The other chains start from E or S.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D13", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Management sets the objective \"achieve an ESG score of 90\". The best evaluation?</p>", "options": [["A", "Ideal, because scores measure sustainability exactly"], ["B", "Risky: optimising the score can replace real improvement; the objective should be managing material risks and creating long-term value, with the score as an outcome"], ["C", "Irrelevant, because scores have no effect"], ["D", "Acceptable only for banks"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Thermometer ≠ health. Good ESG performance → rating, not rating → pretend performance.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D14", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A miner reports its high emissions accurately and sets a modest, verified reduction target. Is this greenwashing?</p>", "options": [["A", "Yes, because emissions are high"], ["B", "Yes, because the target is modest"], ["C", "No: weak performance disclosed honestly is not greenwashing, which requires claims exceeding reality"], ["D", "No, because mining is exempt"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Greenwashing is about misleading or unsupported communication, not poor performance itself.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D15", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A food manufacturer pledges carbon neutrality but lacks an emissions baseline, has a supplier accused of forced labour, no board ESG oversight and EBITDA-only bonuses. Best judgement?</p>", "options": [["A", "Credible, because the pledge is public"], ["B", "Not credible yet: no baseline, social supply-chain exposure and weak governance mean the claim exceeds evidence, creating greenwashing risk"], ["C", "Irrelevant to ERM"], ["D", "Credible if marketing approves it"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Evaluate against baseline, measurability, pathway, ownership, investment, supplier inclusion, consequences and reporting. Here governance, data and incentives are all missing.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Auditor", "question": "<p>A company produces an excellent sustainability report but has weak internal sustainability controls. The auditor's conclusion?</p>", "options": [["A", "Strong ESG risk management, because reporting is excellent"], ["B", "Reporting and risk management are related but not identical; weak controls mean the reported information and risk management are both unreliable"], ["C", "No issue, because reports are voluntary"], ["D", "The report should be shortened"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Reporting asks \"what do we disclose?\"; risk management asks \"what do we do?\". Reliable data comes from managed risks and controls.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Board", "question": "<p>A board wants to copy PETRONAS's ESG strategy for its bank. The best advice?</p>", "options": [["A", "Copy it exactly; PETRONAS is a leader"], ["B", "Adopt all 17 SDGs equally"], ["C", "Tailor ESG to the bank's own material exposures, such as governance, data privacy and financed activities"], ["D", "Focus only on office energy use"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>ESG strategy should fit actual exposure, as IOI does by mapping only relevant SDGs, echoing ISO 31000's \"tailored\" principle.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>Critique: \"An ESG index inclusion proves a firm is sustainable.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Valid: indices measure sustainability directly"], ["B", "Valid for Malaysian firms only"], ["C", "Invalid: an index is a classification or filter based on defined criteria; it is an outcome signal, not sustainability itself"], ["D", "Invalid, because indices are illegal"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Index = classification/benchmark/filter. Treating it as the goal invites metric optimisation over real risk reduction.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Should a firm prioritise all 120 ESG indicators it can find equally?</p>", "options": [["A", "Yes, completeness is always best"], ["B", "Yes, regulators require it"], ["C", "No, ESG should be ignored"], ["D", "No: materiality focuses resources on the factors capable of meaningfully affecting objectives and value"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Without materiality, ESG becomes an infinite checklist. Risk management requires prioritisation.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D16", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which order should a manufacturer follow to seek FTSE4Good inclusion and sustainable financing credibly?</p>", "options": [["A", "Publish a report → buy a rating → assess suppliers later"], ["B", "Set targets → publish → establish governance"], ["C", "Marketing agency → green photos → 80-page PDF"], ["D", "Governance → materiality → data → baseline → targets → controls → performance → disclosure"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Governance and data come before reporting; otherwise the firm wanders into greenwashing. Index inclusion should follow as an outcome.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D17", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which set of KRIs best fits a manufacturer's material ESG risks?</p>", "options": [["A", "Share price and dividend yield only"], ["B", "Energy intensity and GHG emissions; injury rate and supplier labour violations; control breaches and ESG issues escalated to the board"], ["C", "Number of sustainability photos in the annual report"], ["D", "The ESG rating only"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>KRIs should be early-warning indicators tied to material exposures across E, S and G, not outcome scores or cosmetic counts.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D18", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>How should ESG risk management be positioned inside the organisation?</p>", "options": [["A", "As a separate department with its own independent risk process"], ["B", "Within marketing"], ["C", "Inside ERM: material ESG issues are drivers feeding strategic, operational, market, reputation, compliance and liquidity risk processes"], ["D", "Outsourced entirely to rating agencies"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>ERM ⊃ material ESG risks. ESG provides drivers and indicators for existing processes; Topic 4 extends ERM rather than replacing it.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Board", "question": "<p>Which board mechanism most reduces greenwashing risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "A larger marketing budget"], ["B", "Board oversight of sustainability claims: data ownership, pre-publication verification and accountability for disclosures"], ["C", "More ESG awards"], ["D", "Removing all sustainability disclosure"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Greenwashing is largely a governance failure; verification and accountability close the claim-reality gap. Silence forgoes legitimate value and may breach reporting expectations.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Design an ESG risk programme for a listed company. Which architecture fits Topic 4 best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Business objectives → material ESG issues → risk assessment → governance and owners → baselines, metrics and KRIs → targets, controls, responses → monitoring → disclosure → review"], ["B", "Disclosure → rating → targets → governance"], ["C", "Ratings agency → marketing → report"], ["D", "Targets only, without baselines"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>This is the create-level architecture in the notes: governance and data before disclosure, with continuous review. Ratings should follow, not lead.</p>"}];
